#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Purpose: Executable reference implementation of the `/ds-cleanup-worktrees`
         predicate (DS-worktree-reaper). As of the round-9 command-file
         collapse, `content/commands/ds-cleanup-worktrees.md` Step 2
         invokes this tool directly instead of hand-spelling the predicate
         as prose - closing the single largest confirmed cause of worktree
         accumulation (33 non-root worktrees measured in this repo alone,
         oldest from 2026-07-09, back when nothing in the codebase invoked
         the predicate automatically at all; see the ticket brief for the
         full diagnosis). This tool is also wired into `bin/ds-base-sync`
         (advisory --count-only) and a SessionStart nudge (--count-only),
         in addition to remaining operator-invocable directly.

         Round-2 Skeptic (1 Critical, 6 Major) rewrite. The v1 removal
         predicate was a bespoke, second copy of evidence semantics that
         shadowed the shared normative model and was strictly MORE
         permissive than it (branch-gone-from-origin and PR-CLOSED were
         both treated as sufficient alone). This version deletes that
         second copy entirely and delegates the LOCKED/DIRTY/branch-
         evidence decision to `bin/tests/worktree_model.py`'s
         `disposition_for` - the same function `content/commands/
         ds-cleanup-worktrees.md` Step 2 now invokes this tool to run,
         rather than restating - so this tool can no longer diverge from
         it. See "Removal predicate" below for the full gate order,
         including three additional safety floors `disposition_for` does
         not know about (self-worktree, age, and gitignored-content).

Classification reuses `bin/tests/worktree_model.py`'s `classify_entry`
(path-prefix classification: `.claude/worktrees/` = ISOLATION,
`.agentic/worktrees/` = CONDUCTOR_CREATED, anything else = UNMANAGED) - the
single normative definition, never restated here. `evals/.worktrees/*`
(pinned per repo decision #203) already classifies UNMANAGED under that
model and is never touched by this tool regardless.

Removal predicate (worktree REMOVAL only - `git worktree remove`, never
`git branch -D`; a worktree's branch and its commits survive removal).
Gate order, first match wins, ALL must clear for REMOVE:
  1. UNMANAGED / MAIN -> never touched (classification-level exclusion,
     before any of the gates below run at all).
  2. Directory-missing admin entry -> SKIP_MISSING / SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED
     (handled by `git worktree prune` at the end of the run, per the
     existing session-start-prune unlock-then-prune pattern - see the
     Locked handling note below for the one case where an unlock fires).
  3. SELF (new, round-2 Major 2a) -> a worktree whose directory the
     CURRENT PROCESS is running inside (compared via `Path.cwd()`) is
     NEVER removed, unconditionally, before any other check. This is a
     hard floor, not a heuristic: a v1 dry run against the live 38-
     worktree checkout flagged this tool's OWN worktree as REMOVE.
  4. AGE FLOOR (new, round-2 Major 2b) -> a worktree whose OWN ROOT
     DIRECTORY's mtime (an approximate proxy, re-armed by any operation
     touching the path directly - never the branch's or its commits'
     creation time) is within `--min-age-hours` (default 24) is never
     removed, regardless of dirty/lock/branch state. This protects a
     resumable-idle worktree - the harness releases its lock once an agent
     finishes a TURN, not once the session is truly done; an unlocked,
     clean worktree can still belong to work the operator will resume. See
     the Locked handling note below for why this floor exists
     independently of `SKIP_LOCKED`, not instead of it.
  4.5. RECENT ACTIVITY (DS-196) -> `SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY`, immediately
     after the age floor, before any git/gh evidence is gathered. Unlike
     gate 4's single directory-mtime stat, this walks every FILE under the
     worktree (pruning `.git`/`node_modules`/`.venv`/`dist`/`build`/
     `.next`/`__pycache__` - see `_worktree_activity_hours`) and gates on
     the most recent FILE mtime being within `--activity-window-hours`
     (default 3.0) - a more direct liveness signal layered on top of gate
     4's approximate directory-mtime proxy. An indeterminate reading
     (`None`) fails CLOSED, same posture as gate 4. `SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY`
     masks `SKIP_DIRTY`/`SKIP_LOCKED` in `--explain` for an in-use tree,
     same class as `SKIP_TOO_YOUNG` masking those same two gates.
  5. LOCKED (directory present) -> `disposition_for`'s own gate;
     `SKIP_LOCKED`, unconditionally, never unlocked or force-removed here.
  6. DIRTY -> `disposition_for`'s own gate (`git status --porcelain`,
     computed together with the ignored-content check below in one call).
  7. PROTECTED CONTENT (round-2 Major 3, round-3 OPERATOR DECISION on
     polarity, round-4 correction inside `.agentic/`) -> gitignored
     content is a PROTECTED DENYLIST by default, not a fail-safe
     allowlist: a path blocks removal ONLY when it matches
     `_PROTECTED_CONTENT_DIR_PREFIXES`/`_PROTECTED_CONTENT_BASENAME_PATTERNS`
     below (`docs/planning/**`, `.env*`, `*.local`) -
     `SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT`, reporting the offending paths. Everything
     else ignored, INCLUDING generated adapter output (`.kimi/`, `.codex/`,
     `.claude/skills/`) and the round-2 ephemeral set (`node_modules/`,
     `dist/`, etc.), is disposable and does NOT block - EXCEPT
     `.agentic/**`, which flips to the OPPOSITE polarity (see
     `_is_agentic_disposable`'s docstring): protected by default inside
     `.agentic/`, disposable ONLY for a small named set of routine
     telemetry/generated-subdir/cache paths. Round 2 shipped the round-3
     polarity's opposite (a fail-safe allowlist everywhere) and measured
     `removed=0`; round 3 then blanket-protected `.agentic/**` and
     ALSO measured `removed=0`, because this repo dogfoods its own
     methodology and every worktree that has ever hosted an agent
     accumulates routine `.agentic/events.jsonl` telemetry. Round 4 is the
     correction: `.agentic/**` was only ever meant to protect AUTHORED
     work (plans, notes, decisions), not routine session telemetry. The
     round-2/round-3 allowlist behavior (for everything, including
     `.agentic/`) remains reachable via `--strict-ignored`, UNCHANGED by
     round 4. `.agentic/plan.md` (or any other still-protected `.agentic/`
     path) is the highest-value entry, proven empirically: a worktree
     holding only that file reports a CLEAN `git status --porcelain` (no
     `--ignored` flag) and a non-force `git worktree remove` destroys it
     silently. Before any REMOVE actually runs, `.agentic/events.jsonl`
     (if present) is salvaged into the primary repo's
     `.agentic/reaped-telemetry/` first - see "Telemetry salvage" below;
     telemetry is disposable for the purpose of BLOCKING removal, but it
     is not worthless, and a failed salvage blocks removal too.
  8. Branch evidence -> delegated entirely to `disposition_for` (ancestry,
     then content-subsumption, then a bare MERGED PR, then origin-
     reachability (DS-196), then push status - `strict_pr_state=False`,
     the same lenient grade the shared model already reserves for
     worktree removal, since removal does not destroy commits). A
     never-pushed branch with unique commits, or a CLOSED PR, both resolve
     `SKIP_*` (never `ELIGIBLE`) under this model - NOT sufficient alone,
     unlike v1's bespoke predicate. DS-196's `origin_reachable` evidence
     (`_compute_origin_reachable`, local-only) resolves `ELIGIBLE` when
     this entry's branch tip is reachable from ANY `origin/*` ref AND
     `pr_state` was affirmatively resolved (not `"not_checked"`) -
     `--no-origin-reachable-evidence` restores pre-DS-196 evidence order
     exactly. See `worktree_model.py`'s `WORKTREE_REMOVAL_EVIDENCE_ORDER`
     and `_check_origin_reachable` docstrings for the full precedence
     rationale and safety precondition.
  9. PR-QUERY-FAILURE OVERRIDE (round-N Major fix) -> a `gh` PR-state query
     that could not be answered at all (nonzero exit, unparseable JSON, or
     a timeout - see `_pr_state`'s docstring) is a DISTINCT fact from "no
     PR exists" and must never be collapsed into it. When such a failure
     occurred AND the resulting disposition is exactly `SKIP_NOT_PUSHED` or
     `SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR` - the two dispositions `--archive-unproven`'s
     whitelist below would otherwise treat as fully resolved - the entry is
     reclassified to its own `SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR` outcome, never
     `SKIP_UNPROVEN`, so it is excluded from that whitelist by
     construction, not merely by an unlisted `reason` value. Mirrors the
     `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR` precedent for the ls-remote leg.
`ELIGIBLE` from `disposition_for`, with no protected-content offenders and
no unresolved PR-query failure, is the only path to `REMOVE`. Every other
outcome is reported, never a force-delete on an inconclusive read.

Telemetry salvage (round-4 requirement, non-`--dry-run` only; round-6
correction: applies identically on BOTH removal paths): immediately
before `git worktree remove` runs for an entry - whether via the plain
REMOVE loop or via `--archive-unproven`'s archive-then-remove loop -
`<path>/.agentic/events.jsonl` (if present and non-empty) is copied into
`<repo>/.agentic/reaped-telemetry/<branch>-<timestamp>.jsonl` and the copy
is VERIFIED to have actually landed non-empty - `ds-cost`/retro read this
data, so it must survive the worktree's own deletion even though it does
not block removal on its own. Both loops call the SAME `_salvage_and_remove`
function for this sequence, so they cannot diverge on this guarantee again
(round-5 shipped the archive loop calling `git worktree remove` directly,
bypassing salvage entirely - fixed in round 6). ANY salvage failure
(unreadable source, uncreatable destination directory, copy error, or
failed post-copy verification) blocks the remove entirely for that entry -
a failed salvage must never become a silent deletion of the data it was
trying to preserve. On the plain REMOVE loop this reclassifies the entry to
`SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT`; on the archive loop the entry stays
`SKIP_UNPROVEN` (its already-created, already-verified bundle is left in
place - a failed salvage does not undo a successful archive, it only
withholds the removal). `--dry-run` skips salvage entirely (nothing is
copied) but still reports how many files WOULD be salvaged.
`.agentic/reaped-telemetry/` needs no new `.gitignore` carve-out: the
repo's existing `/.agentic/*`-style umbrella already covers any new
top-level entry under `.agentic/`.

Archiving unproven branches (round-5 requirement, `--archive-unproven`,
OPT-IN, NEVER the default; round-6 correction to the eligible set and to
degraded-mode handling): round 4's `SKIP_UNPROVEN` verdict on a
never-pushed branch with real commits is correct and this tool does not
weaken it - nothing about the predicate above changes. The gap is that
nothing ever RESOLVES that class on its own, which is why worktrees
accumulate indefinitely even after every other gate is passing. This repo
already solved the identical problem for BRANCHES (a 2026-08-11 manual
one-off operator sweep archived 75 unprovable branches into one verified
bundle before deleting them, DS-153/`.agentic/branch-archive/` -
`bin/ds-branch-prune` itself does not call `git bundle`) -
`--archive-unproven` extends that exact pattern to WORKTREES, but only
to a narrow, EXPLICIT whitelist
within `SKIP_UNPROVEN`, not the whole bucket. Round 5 shipped a filter on
the `SKIP_UNPROVEN` outcome bucket alone, which silently swept in
`SKIP_PR_OPEN` (a hard safety override - an OPEN PR is not "unresolved
branch content", it is affirmative evidence the branch is still live work)
and `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR` (a transient network failure, not proof of
anything) - the "branch content itself is the ONLY unresolved question"
claim this paragraph used to make was therefore false as written. Round 6
fixes this: `--archive-unproven` archives ONLY entries whose underlying
disposition (preserved in the entry's own `reason` field, and visible via
`--explain`) is exactly `SKIP_NOT_PUSHED` or `SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR` -
`_ARCHIVABLE_UNPROVEN_DISPOSITIONS`, an explicit named whitelist so a new
disposition added to `worktree_model.py` later is excluded by default,
never swept in automatically. `SKIP_PR_OPEN` and `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR`
(and any other `SKIP_UNPROVEN`-bucketed disposition not on the whitelist)
are NEVER archived, unconditionally, even with the flag set. Separately,
`--no-gh` (or `gh` simply being unavailable/unauthenticated) degrades
EVERY entry's PR-state leg to inconclusive, which would otherwise make
`SKIP_PR_OPEN` entries indistinguishable from genuinely-unprovable ones -
`--archive-unproven` therefore REFUSES to run at all (prints an error to
stderr and exits 1, doing no work) whenever gh evidence is unavailable or
degraded; it never silently downgrades to a more permissive archive pass.
That process-level refusal covers total `gh` unavailability (missing,
unauthenticated, `--no-gh`), but NOT a transient PER-ENTRY query failure -
`gh auth status` can succeed while a single `gh pr list` call for one
specific branch still errors (rate limit, network blip). Round-N Major fix:
such a failure is a distinct fact from "no PR exists" (see `_pr_state`'s
docstring) and, when it would otherwise leave an entry resolved to exactly
`SKIP_NOT_PUSHED` or `SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR`, `evaluate_entry` reclassifies
it to its own `SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR` outcome (Removal predicate gate 9,
above) - a bucket that is never `SKIP_UNPROVEN` at all, so it is excluded
from `_ARCHIVABLE_UNPROVEN_DISPOSITIONS` by construction, not merely by an
unlisted `reason` value. Without this, a worktree behind a genuinely OPEN
PR whose query happened to fail on this run would be indistinguishable from
a genuinely-unprovable branch and could be silently archived and removed.
For every entry that clears the whitelist (already clean, unlocked, past
the age floor, not self, not protected-content, past the gh-evidence
refusal check - i.e. the ONLY thing blocking it is exactly one of the two
whitelisted unprovable-branch-evidence dispositions): (1) `git bundle
create <repo>/.agentic/worktree-archive/<branch>-<timestamp>.bundle
<branch>` captures the FULL branch (every commit unique to it, not just
its tip) - when the resolved base branch's objects can be excluded
without producing an empty bundle, the exclusion is applied (DS-191),
yielding a small bundle plus a recorded prerequisite commit rather than
a full copy of history already present on the base. An explicit --base
is only ever eligible for this exclusion when it resolves to the same
commit this tool's own auto-resolution would have picked (round-N,
MAJOR-2 fix - see `_archive_compaction_base`'s docstring); an arbitrary
caller-supplied ref that verifies fine but isn't durable is never
compacted against, full-history only; (2) the bundle is
VERIFIED with `git bundle verify` BEFORE any removal - a create failure, a
missing/empty bundle, or a failed verification leaves the entry
`SKIP_UNPROVEN`, reported, worktree untouched (same discipline as the
telemetry-salvage guard above: a failed archive must never become a
silent deletion of the only copy of unproven work); (3) telemetry is then
salvaged (see "Telemetry salvage" below - this loop runs the identical
`_salvage_and_remove` sequence the plain REMOVE loop runs, never a second
copy of it) and, only once salvage clears, the worktree is removed,
reclassified to `ARCHIVED_AND_REMOVED` (its own reported bucket,
`archived-and-removed=N`, keeping the bucket-sum-equals-entries invariant
intact) and the exact restore command (`git fetch <bundle>
"refs/heads/<branch>:refs/heads/<branch>"` - braced, per this repo's own
documented zsh-mangling gotcha) is printed. A salvage failure at step (3)
leaves the bundle from step (1)/(2) in place (already verified, already
safe) but does NOT remove the worktree - the entry stays `SKIP_UNPROVEN`,
reported. This tool NEVER deletes the branch itself, only the worktree -
reclaiming the archived branch's own local ref remains
`bin/ds-branch-prune`'s job, same as it already is for every other path in
this tool. `--dry-run` creates no bundle but reports the would-be count
(computed against the SAME whitelist). `.agentic/worktree-archive/` needs
no new `.gitignore` carve-out (same umbrella as
`.agentic/reaped-telemetry/`) and grows unbounded - pruning it is the
operator's own responsibility, exactly like `.agentic/branch-archive/`.

Public API (CLI):
  ds-cleanup-worktrees [--repo <path>]... [root ...] [--multi-repo] [--depth N]
                    [--report [--json]] [--base <ref>] [--dry-run] [--no-gh]
                    [--explain] [--count-only] [--min-age-hours <float>]
                    [--activity-window-hours <float>]
                    [--no-origin-reachable-evidence]
                    [--strict-ignored] [--archive-unproven] [--max-repos N]
                    [--measure-size]
  ds-cleanup-worktrees --init-config

  --repo <path>          Repository to operate on. Default: cwd. Repeatable
                          ONLY when `--multi-repo` is also given - a bare
                          `--repo <path>` (no `--multi-repo`) behaves exactly
                          as before this flag existed: `resolved = args.repo[0]
                          if args.repo else "."`. Passing `--repo` more than
                          once WITHOUT `--multi-repo` is a usage error (exit
                          2, before any git subprocess call) - this preserves
                          byte-identical behavior for every existing caller
                          (bin/ds-base-sync, hooks/session-start-wrap.sh,
                          this command's own doc), none of which pass it more
                          than once.
  --multi-repo            (round-N, DS-cleanup-worktrees-multi-repo Unit 2)
                          Enables cross-repo mode: `--repo` becomes
                          repeatable, bare positional `root ...` arguments
                          are accepted (each root's children are scanned to
                          `--depth` levels for git repos - a `.git` dir or
                          file at that level - non-git directories skipped
                          silently, nothing beneath a directory once
                          identified as a repo is scanned further), and a
                          config-file fallback at `~/.agentic/
                          cleanup-worktrees.json` (shape `{"roots": [...],
                          "repos": [...]}`, either key optional, consulted
                          ONLY when neither an explicit `--repo` nor a
                          positional root was given at all). Explicit
                          `--repo` entries and positional
                          roots are ADDITIVE explicit sources - both are
                          honored when both are given, deduped by canonical
                          resolved path (first-seen-wins). Every repo is
                          swept SEQUENTIALLY, never in parallel - legible
                          output, no concurrent `git`/`gh` rate-limit
                          contention across repos - a concern sharpened by
                          `--archive-unproven`, which makes per-entry `gh pr
                          list` calls too. `root ...` positional arguments
                          or `--depth` given WITHOUT `--multi-repo` is a
                          usage error (exit 2). `--base` combined with
                          `--multi-repo` is a usage error (exit 2) - each
                          repo ALWAYS resolves its own base independently via
                          `resolve_base_branch(repo, None)`, per that repo's
                          own AGENTS.md declaration or automatic tier order;
                          a single global `--base` value would silently leak
                          one repo's base into every other repo, which is
                          exactly the isolation bug this mode must not
                          reintroduce. Multi-repo exit codes: 0 when every
                          repo/root was clean; 1 when at least one repo or
                          root had a discovery or evaluation error (one
                          failure never halts the sweep - every other
                          repo/root is still attempted) - a repo whose base
                          could not be resolved counts as this case too
                          (round-N Major 1 fix; agrees with `--report`,
                          which already exits 1 on the identical condition
                          via `_deep_report_row`'s own `err` path), reported
                          in its own `skipped-base-unresolved=N` summary
                          field rather than being silently folded into
                          `swept`; 2 on a usage error, INCLUDING zero repos
                          discovered with zero root errors (no `--repo`, no
                          roots, no usable config).
                          This binary has no separate `--timeout` flag: since
                          discovery and sweep run in-process rather than as a
                          subprocess-per-repo, there is no outer process to
                          wedge as a whole. Every network-touching call this
                          binary makes (`git ls-remote`, `gh auth status`,
                          `gh pr list`) already carries its own
                          `_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` bound (see that constant
                          above), so no single call can hang unboundedly.
                          This is NARROWER parity than the retired flag, not
                          full parity: the old `--timeout` also gave an
                          operator-settable AGGREGATE per-repo ceiling, and
                          that aggregate cap has no replacement here - a repo
                          with N worktree entries is bounded only at
                          `_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` times (up to 2 network
                          calls per entry, plus 1 per repo for `gh auth
                          status`), with no overall per-repo ceiling -
                          verified against the code, not merely asserted.
  root ...                Positional root directories (requires
                          `--multi-repo`; see that flag's description
                          above for the full scan/dedup/config-fallback
                          semantics).
  --depth N                Scan depth from each root (requires
                          `--multi-repo`). Default 1 (immediate children
                          only). Range 1-3.
  --report                 (requires `--multi-repo`; a single-repo report is
                          just `--dry-run --explain` and needs no dedicated
                          flag) Ranked cross-repo visibility - the
                          "which project is worst" half of this feature,
                          distinct from the sweep half above. STRUCTURALLY
                          READ-ONLY: this mode never calls
                          `_salvage_and_remove`, `git worktree remove`,
                          `git worktree unlock`, or `git worktree prune`,
                          regardless of `--dry-run` - there is no removal
                          code path to reach at all, not merely a
                          `--dry-run`-shaped one. Incompatible with
                          `--archive-unproven` (usage error, exit 2 - report
                          mode never archives or removes anything, so the
                          combination is meaningless). Two cost tiers,
                          selected by whether `--count-only` is ALSO given:
                            - `--report --count-only` (FAST/approximate):
                              exactly 2 git calls per repo (`rev-parse
                              --show-toplevel`, `worktree list
                              --porcelain`), zero network calls. Ranks repos
                              by `(nonroot_count desc, oldest_worktree_age_
                              hours desc)` - on a count tie, the repo whose
                              OLDEST worktree has accumulated the MOST age
                              ranks worse (round-N operator decision,
                              correcting the original spec: the initial
                              `asc` tiebreak ranked the repo whose oldest
                              worktree was YOUNGER as the worse offender on
                              a tie, which is backwards for "which project
                              is worst" - older accumulation is worse, not
                              better). Age is the ENUMERATED worktree
                              directory's own `mtime` via `os.stat` (free,
                              no extra subprocess). This tier is a WEAK
                              proxy: a project with 30 legitimately-active
                              worktrees is not the same as one with 30 stale
                              ones - the printed table and JSON both label
                              this tier explicitly so an operator reading a
                              fast-tier result knows what it does and does
                              not tell them. A SECOND, separate caveat
                              (round-N Minor 2): this tier never calls
                              `resolve_base_branch` at all, so a repo whose
                              base cannot be resolved (e.g. no git remotes -
                              see `_has_any_remote`) still exits 0 here and
                              ranks as ordinary, actionable output, even
                              though the DEEP tier and the real sweep both
                              exit 1 and skip that same repo. An operator
                              following the "run --count-only first"
                              recommendation below can see a clean-looking
                              table that overstates what is actually
                              reapable - cross-check against a DEEP
                              `--report` (or the sweep's own exit code)
                              before trusting a fast-tier row as reapable.
                            - `--report` (DEEP/accurate): a full per-entry
                              `evaluate_entry` pass per repo - the same cost
                              as `--dry-run --explain` today, just never
                              acting on the result. Ranks repos by
                              `(eligible desc, oldest_worktree_age_hours
                              desc)` - the count of entries that would
                              resolve `REMOVE` (`eligible`), with the SAME
                              oldest-age-DESC tiebreak the fast tier uses
                              (round-N Minor 3: previously an `eligible` tie
                              fell back to undocumented discovery order).
                          Recommended as the FIRST step before any
                          multi-repo removal run - look before you delete.
  --json                   (requires `--report`) Emits the ranked report to
                          stdout as JSON in place of the table:
                          `{"tier": "fast"|"deep", "rows":
                          [{"repo": str, "nonroot_worktrees": int,
                          "oldest_age_hours": float|null, "eligible":
                          int|null}, ...], "truncated": bool}` (round-N Major 5 fix: the
                          printed table already labels its tier explicitly
                          - see the fast/deep banner lines above - and the
                          top-level `tier` field gives a machine consumer
                          the identical signal the table already gives a
                          human; the shape is new in this unpushed unit, so
                          there is no compatibility burden in changing it
                          from a bare array). `eligible` is `null` ONLY
                          under the fast (`--count-only`) tier, which never
                          computes it; `oldest_age_hours` is populated in
                          BOTH tiers (the deep tier computes it from the
                          same `entries[1:]` list it already enumerates for
                          `evaluate_entry`, at no extra subprocess cost) and
                          is `null` only in the genuinely-unreachable
                          zero-entries case. `truncated` is `true` iff `--max-repos` cut the
                          discovered-and-deduped set (see `--max-repos` below), `false`
                          otherwise - `rows` never carries an entry for a truncated-away repo,
                          so a consumer must check this key before treating `rows`' length as
                          the true discovered count. Never written to `.agentic/hud/*` - that store is
                          per-worker orchestration telemetry with a
                          different consumer and lifecycle than an
                          operator-invoked report.
  --base <ref>           Base ref to prove ancestor-of-base evidence
                          against. Default: None - when omitted, resolved
                          automatically via `resolve_base_branch` (see that
                          function's docstring for the full tier order:
                          AGENTS.md's `BASE_BRANCH:` declaration - which is
                          AUTHORITATIVE, failing resolution outright rather
                          than falling through if declared but unresolvable
                          - then `origin/HEAD`, then a local develop/
                          development branch, then main falling back to
                          master, with tiers after the AGENTS.md
                          declaration each validated against origin and
                          skipped with a diagnostic on failure). Passing
                          --base explicitly
                          uses it verbatim, no validation or fallthrough -
                          this preserves the tool's pre-existing precedence
                          for an operator override. Round-4 rework: this
                          replaces `content/commands/ds-cleanup-worktrees.md`
                          Step 2's former hand-rolled grep/awk/sed
                          text-extraction pipeline, which produced a fresh
                          defect in three consecutive review rounds (prose
                          false-match, quote/prefix mishandling, fence-
                          stripper misses, trailing-whitespace survival,
                          missing validation fallthrough) - tested Python
                          instead of a shell pipeline nobody could
                          exhaustively regression-test.
  --dry-run              Compute and report; remove nothing.
  --no-gh                Force degraded mode: skip BOTH the PR-state leg
                          AND `git ls-remote` (round-2 Major 4 - v1's
                          --no-gh disabled only `gh`, so a "--no-gh" run
                          still made one network round-trip per entry via
                          `ls-remote`; measured 14.94s / ~31 entries on the
                          live checkout). Genuinely offline: zero network
                          calls of any kind. Degrades those legs to
                          inconclusive - can only remove FEWER worktrees
                          than a full run, never more.
  --explain               Print one line per worktree entry with its
                          outcome (default: aggregate counts only).
  --count-only            Print ONLY `entries=N` (round-2 Major 4) - zero
                          network calls AND zero per-entry `git` calls
                          beyond the single `git worktree list --porcelain`
                          already required to enumerate. This is the mode
                          `bin/ds-base-sync`'s advisory note and
                          `hooks/session-start-wrap.sh`'s SessionStart
                          nudge both use - neither needs anything beyond a
                          count. Measured 55ms via `time python3
                          bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees --repo <repo> --count-only`
                          against this repo's live 37-worktree checkout on
                          2026-08-11 (replaces the FIRST-round `--dry-run
                          --no-gh` figure of 14.94s against 31 entries on
                          the same checkout, which still made one `git
                          ls-remote` network round-trip per entry - see
                          bin/ds-base-sync and hooks/session-start-wrap.sh's
                          own manifests for that pre-fix figure).
  --min-age-hours <float> Age floor in hours (see gate 4 above). Default
                          24.
  --activity-window-hours <float>
                          DS-196: recent-file-activity floor in hours (see
                          gate 4.5 above). Default 3.0. `evaluate_entry`
                          fires on `activity_hours is None or
                          activity_hours < window`, so the `None` branch
                          (no file could be stat'd inside the worktree -
                          see `_worktree_activity_hours`) still fails
                          CLOSED (SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY) at 0, same as at
                          any other window value, because the `is None`
                          check short-circuits the `or` before `<` ever
                          runs against it
                          (a real `None < 0` comparison raises TypeError in
                          Python 3, not False) - never used as the
                          default; only the age floor runs at 0 seconds'
                          notice, and this floor stays a separate, later
                          gate.
  --no-origin-reachable-evidence
                          DS-196: rollback lever - restores pre-DS-196
                          evidence order exactly (passes
                          `worktree_model.MERGE_EVIDENCE_ORDER`, without
                          `origin_reachable`, to `disposition_for`). Surfaced
                          here and in the docs as an explicit escape hatch,
                          same spirit as `--strict-ignored` below.
  --strict-ignored        Round-3 escape hatch back to the round-2 default:
                          gate 7 (PROTECTED CONTENT) becomes a fail-safe
                          ALLOWLIST again - block unless every ignored path
                          is covered by `_IGNORED_ALLOWLIST_*` (see gate 7
                          above and `_PROTECTED_CONTENT_DIR_PREFIXES`'s own
                          comment for the operator-decision rationale this
                          flag exists to preserve). Applies UNIFORMLY,
                          including inside `.agentic/` - round 4's
                          inverted-polarity disposable set
                          (`_AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_*`) is a `--strict-ignored`
                          bypass mechanism this flag does not use at all;
                          under `--strict-ignored` `.agentic/events.jsonl`
                          blocks removal exactly like round 2, unchanged.
  --archive-unproven      OPT-IN, NEVER the default (see "Archiving
                          unproven branches" above for the full mechanism
                          and rationale). For every entry whose underlying
                          disposition is exactly `SKIP_NOT_PUSHED` or
                          `SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR` (the explicit whitelist -
                          NEVER `SKIP_PR_OPEN`, NEVER
                          `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR`, and never any other
                          `SKIP_UNPROVEN`-bucketed disposition, even
                          though `--explain` reports all of them under the
                          same `SKIP_UNPROVEN` outcome) - archives the full
                          branch (every commit unique to it) into a
                          verified `git bundle` under
                          `.agentic/worktree-archive/`, excluding objects
                          already on the resolved base branch when doing
                          so still yields a non-empty bundle (a compact
                          bundle then requires the recorded prerequisite
                          commit - the fork-point with the base, not
                          necessarily the base's own tip - to remain
                          present locally to restore; a NOTE is printed
                          to stderr when compaction was not possible). An
                          explicit `--base` is only compacted against
                          when it resolves to the same commit
                          auto-resolution would have picked - an
                          unvalidated caller-supplied ref could otherwise
                          be deleted later, permanently orphaning the
                          bundle's prerequisite. Then salvages telemetry,
                          then removes the worktree.
                          Removes WORKTREES ONLY, never branches
                          (`bin/ds-branch-prune` remains responsible for
                          branch deletion). A
                          failed bundle create/verify, or a failed
                          telemetry salvage, never removes anything - the
                          entry stays `SKIP_UNPROVEN`. REFUSES to run
                          (prints an error, exits 1, does no work) when gh
                          evidence is unavailable or degraded
                          (`--no-gh`, or `gh` missing/unauthenticated/
                          erroring) - archiving without PR evidence could
                          not reliably distinguish a genuinely-unprovable
                          branch from one behind an open PR. A PER-ENTRY
                          `gh pr list` query failure (auth fine, this one
                          call errored/timed out/rate-limited) is also
                          never archived: such an entry is reclassified to
                          `SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR` before this whitelist check
                          ever runs (Removal predicate gate 9), so it is
                          never `SKIP_UNPROVEN` in the first place.
  --max-repos N            (DS-189 Unit A, requires `--multi-repo`) Caps the
                          discovered-and-deduped target list to the first N
                          entries (discovery order preserved, no re-sort) -
                          applied AFTER dedup, BEFORE any per-repo
                          evaluation, so it bounds git-call COST rather than
                          just display: an over-limit run pays for exactly
                          N repos' worth of `git`/`gh` calls, never more.
                          Default: unset (unbounded, byte-identical to
                          pre-DS-189 behavior). Under `--report --json`,
                          sets the top-level `"truncated"` key to `true` iff
                          the discovered-and-deduped count exceeded N;
                          `false` when unset or the count did not exceed N.
  --measure-size           OPT-IN, NEVER the default, and NEVER passed by an
                          automatic caller (`bin/ds-base-sync`, the
                          SessionStart nudge, `automation/
                          dinostack-worktree-reap/run.sh` all invoke this
                          tool without it) - DS-216. Measuring means a
                          recursive `du -sk` walk (see `_measure_size_kb`)
                          over potentially tens of GB, and this tool has
                          automatic/scheduled callers whose cost profile
                          must not change. When passed, every REMOVE-bucket
                          entry is measured BEFORE removal (there is
                          nothing left to measure after) and every
                          `SKIP_DIRTY` entry is measured too (dirty
                          worktrees are refused removal terminally by
                          design and are where the measured byte mass on a
                          real checkout actually lives). The summary line
                          gains `would_reclaim_kb` under `--dry-run` or
                          `reclaimed_kb` on a live run (NEVER the same
                          label in both - they mean different things: a
                          projection vs. bytes actually freed), plus
                          `dirty_unreclaimable_kb` (the summed size of
                          `SKIP_DIRTY` entries - NEVER folded into either
                          reclaim total; reporting refused bytes as
                          reclaimed would be a lie in the exact direction
                          this flag exists to prevent) and
                          `size_unknown_count` (entries a `du -sk` call
                          could not measure - timeout or failure, EXCLUDED
                          from every total, never defaulted to 0; a
                          nonzero count means every total this run reports
                          is a FLOOR, not an exact figure - see the printed
                          NOTE). `--explain` gains a trailing `size_kb=N` or
                          `size_unknown=true` per entry. Under `--report`
                          (deep tier only - `--report --count-only` never
                          computes a disposition to measure against, so
                          these fields are always `null` there), each row
                          gains the same three fields (`would_reclaim_kb`
                          always - report mode never removes anything, so
                          there is no `reclaimed_kb` variant here;
                          `dirty_unreclaimable_kb`; `size_unknown_count`),
                          `null` when `--measure-size` was not passed, so a
                          multi-repo total is derivable by summing
                          `--report --json` rows. Deliberately excludes any
                          `reclaimable_by_gc_kb`/`git count-objects` figure
                          - considered and rejected, since `size-garbage`
                          counts only invalid/corrupt loose objects, never
                          unreachable-but-valid objects from a deleted ref
                          (measured `0 bytes` across six repos) - it cannot
                          answer the question it appears to answer. Every
                          byte total measures the working-tree checkout
                          only, never the shared git object store (every
                          worktree of a repo shares it) - removing a
                          worktree never shrinks it, and deleting a branch
                          reclaims nothing until `git gc` runs.
  --init-config            Standalone action: usage error, exit 2, when
                          combined with any OTHER flag set to a
                          non-default value. An explicitly-passed value
                          that happens to equal that flag's own default
                          (e.g. `--init-config --min-age-hours 24` when 24
                          is already the default) is indistinguishable
                          from having not passed that flag at all - the
                          comparison in `main()` diffs `args` against a
                          fresh no-args `Namespace` field-by-field, which
                          cannot see how a value arrived, only what it is
                          - and is tolerated rather than rejected. Scaffolds
                          `~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json` for a
                          first-time `--multi-repo` setup: if the file
                          already exists, prints its current contents and a
                          "not overwriting" line and exits 0 - NEVER
                          overwrites; if absent, creates `~/.agentic/` if
                          needed and writes an EMPTY
                          `{"roots": [], "repos": []}` skeleton atomically
                          (temp file + `os.replace`), printing instructions
                          that the SessionStart machine-wide check will not
                          activate until at least one root or repo entry is
                          added (e.g. `"roots":
                          ["~/Documents/Development"]`).

Locked handling: per `content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md`
§Guardrail, `git worktree unlock` may be used ONLY on a worktree whose
directory is already gone (an admin-only entry - its agent cannot still be
running when there is no directory left to run in). This tool honors that
guardrail exactly: a locked entry whose directory still exists is ALWAYS
reported (`SKIP_LOCKED`), NEVER unlocked or force-removed, regardless of
any other check - the harness's lock is cross-session protection this tool
must never override (AGENTS.md: "The live Claude Code harness auto-locks
every `isolation:"worktree"` worktree at creation"). A locked entry whose
directory is already gone is handled by the admin-prune pass (`git
worktree prune`) at the end of every non-dry-run - `git worktree unlock`
fires first only for that directory-missing case, mirroring the session-
start prune script's own unlock-then-prune pattern. Absence of a lock does
NOT prove a worktree is abandoned - the harness releases the lock once an
agent finishes a TURN, and a session can be resumed into an unlocked,
clean worktree later (this is exactly what a round-2 review correction
established: a v1 dry run flagged this very tool's own live worktree as
REMOVE, which SKIP_LOCKED alone would not have caught since it was
unlocked at the time). Gates 3 (SELF) and 4 (AGE FLOOR) above exist
BECAUSE of this gap, not instead of `SKIP_LOCKED` - all three are
independent, additive protections.

Upstream deps: Python 3 stdlib only (argparse, fnmatch, json, os, re, shutil,
               subprocess, sys, time, pathlib, collections, typing). `git`
               (CLI) and, unless --no-gh, `gh` (CLI, for the PR-state leg -
               degrades gracefully when absent or unauthenticated).
               bin/tests/worktree_model.py (WorktreeClass, classify_entry,
               parse_porcelain, Disposition, DispositionFacts,
               disposition_for - imported by path exactly like
               bin/ds-branch-prune does, so a PATH-symlink invocation
               still finds it; disposition_for is the SOLE authority for
               the LOCKED/DIRTY/branch-evidence decision - see the module
               docstring's Removal predicate section for why no second
               copy of that logic exists here after round-2).
               `~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json` (DS-cleanup-worktrees-
               multi-repo Unit 2, OPTIONAL, `--multi-repo` only): the
               config-file fallback for repo/root discovery, consulted
               ONLY when neither an explicit `--repo` nor a positional
               root was given (shape `{"roots": [...], "repos": [...]}`,
               either key optional; malformed JSON, a non-list value, or a
               non-string element is reported to stderr and treated as
               empty for that key - never an uncaught exception) - see
               `--multi-repo`'s own Public API description above and
               `_load_multi_repo_config`/`_config_string_list`.

Downstream consumers: content/commands/ds-cleanup-worktrees.md (Step 2
                      invokes this tool directly - this tool IS the
                      predicate, not a second copy of it; the command file
                      remains the operator-readable prose entry point);
                      bin/ds-base-sync (advisory --count-only report
                      appended to its tail, non-blocking, never mutates -
                      the one guaranteed post-merge trigger point);
                      hooks/session-start-wrap.sh (a non-blocking advisory
                      --count-only nudge line when the non-root worktree
                      count exceeds a small threshold, pointing at
                      `/ds-cleanup-worktrees`; report-only, never removes
                      anything from that call site);
                      content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md's
                      §Session-start prune script (DS-196, MUTATING - a
                      SEPARATE call site from the hook above: a plain,
                      backgrounded `ds-cleanup-worktrees --repo <root>`
                      invocation with no `--count-only`/`--dry-run`, run
                      once per session-start preflight and unattended;
                      suppressible via `AE_WORKTREE_REAP_DISABLE=1` - this
                      IS the automatic call site the Performance section
                      above now refers to);
                      automation/dinostack-worktree-reap/run.sh (DS-189, a
                      sibling unit of this same PR - a scheduled,
                      report-only automation package that consumes
                      `--multi-repo --report --json` for its ranked-repo
                      signal and `--init-config` for first-run setup;
                      structurally read-only, same as `--report` itself).
                      CI: bin/tests/test_cleanup_worktrees.py (single-repo
                      surface, pytest, auto-collected by `python3 -m
                      pytest bin/tests/`) and
                      bin/tests/test_cleanup_worktrees_multi_repo.py
                      (`--multi-repo`/`--report` surface: discovery,
                      config-file fallback, dedup, per-repo base
                      isolation, report read-only guarantee, fast/deep
                      tier cost and ranking, usage errors, mixed-repo
                      exit codes - same auto-collection).

Failure modes: SINGLE-REPO MODE (`--multi-repo` absent) - exits 0 on
               success INCLUDING partial degradation (gh
               unavailable/unauthenticated/erroring/--no-gh/network
               timeout; individual `git worktree remove` failures are
               reported to stderr and the run continues). Exits 1 ONLY on
               an internal/usage error: --repo is not a directory or not a
               git repository, or an unhandled exception while
               enumerating worktrees. This exit-code contract is UNCHANGED
               by `--multi-repo`/`--report` (DS-cleanup-worktrees-multi-repo
               Unit 2) - `bin/ds-base-sync` and
               `hooks/session-start-wrap.sh` depend on it. MULTI-REPO MODE
               (`--multi-repo` given) has its OWN, separate exit contract:
               2 on a usage error (validated before any git subprocess
               call - see `_validate_args`, and the module docstring's
               `--multi-repo` section for the full list, including zero
               repos discovered with zero root errors); 1 when at least
               one repo or root had a discovery or evaluation error, which
               now ALSO includes a repo whose base could not be resolved
               (round-N Major 1 fix - previously silently counted as
               `swept`, contradicting both this contract and `--report`'s
               own identical-condition exit 1); 0 only when every
               discovered repo/root was fully clean. `--report` (also
               requires `--multi-repo`) shares this same 0/1/2 contract via
               `_run_report`'s own `errored`/`root_errors` check - it never
               reaches the removal code path at all (structurally
               read-only), so its exit code reflects discovery/evaluation
               errors only, identically to the sweep path.
               (round-4 rework) When --base is
               omitted, base resolution can fail two ways, both exiting 0
               having done nothing: either AGENTS.md declares a
               `BASE_BRANCH:` whose `origin/<name>` ref does not validate -
               the declaration is AUTHORITATIVE and never falls through to
               a lower tier on failure, so this alone is fatal to
               resolution - or, when no declaration exists, every
               automatic candidate (origin/HEAD, local develop/development,
               main falling back to master) fails `git rev-parse --verify`
               against the local remote-tracking ref (not the remote
               itself - a stale or unfetched ref reads as missing even when
               the branch exists on origin; `git fetch origin` may resolve
               it). Either way `resolve_base_branch` names every
               candidate tried (printed to stderr) and the run exits 0
               having done nothing - a resolved-but-invalid base fails
               SAFE, matching the shell pipeline this replaces (a WARNING,
               never a hard exit, so a caller's subsequent steps still
               run); nothing is ever removed against a guessed-wrong base
               regardless. Never
               removes a dirty, self, too-
               young, locked, or ignored-content-bearing worktree under
               any circumstance. Never removes `evals/.worktrees/*` -
               those classify UNMANAGED under `classify_entry` and are
               always reported, never touched, same as any other
               UNMANAGED entry. Every network-touching subprocess call
               (`git ls-remote`, `gh auth status`, `gh pr list`) is
               wrapped with a timeout (`_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`); a
               timeout degrades that leg to inconclusive exactly like a
               `gh` error does, never a false ELIGIBLE.

Performance: (DS-196 correction: full mode IS now the mode an automatic call
             site uses - `content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md`'s
             session-start reap block invokes plain `ds-cleanup-worktrees
             --repo <root>` with no `--count-only`/`--dry-run`, backgrounded,
             once per session start. Per-entry cost below is unchanged by
             that call site's own concurrency - see
             content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md's "Residual, named
             not fixed (round-2 Minor 4)" note, immediately after the
             session-start prune script, for the one accepted trade-off
             this introduces (a race with the synchronous `ds-branch-prune`
             call that follows it in the same preflight).)
             `--count-only` - two git calls total: `rev-parse
             --show-toplevel` (already paid by repo resolution before this
             mode's own check runs - `_resolve_repo` in single-repo mode,
             `RepoTarget.__init__` under `--multi-repo`) plus `git worktree
             list --porcelain` to enumerate. Zero network calls, and zero
             ADDITIONAL per-entry git calls beyond those two (see the
             --count-only flag description above for the re-measured
             figure) - this agrees with, rather than contradicts, the
             `--report --count-only` tier's own "exactly 2 git calls per
             repo" text above; round-N Major 3 fix corrects this section,
             which previously and self-contradictorily claimed only ONE
             call, mechanically pinned against a live subprocess-call count
             now (see `bin/tests/test_cleanup_worktrees.py`'s
             `test_count_only_git_call_count_matches_manifest`). Full
             mode - the above plus, per
             managed worktree entry: `git status --porcelain
             --ignored=matching`, `git merge-base --is-ancestor`,
             `_worktree_activity_hours`'s recursive `os.walk` of the entry's
             own working tree (DS-196, pruning `_ACTIVITY_SCAN_PRUNE_DIRS` -
             filesystem-bound, not a subprocess, but scales with the
             worktree's own file count), `_compute_origin_reachable`'s `git
             branch -r --contains` (DS-196, local-only, no network), and
             (unless --no-gh) `git ls-remote` + one `gh pr list`, plus a
             final `git worktree prune`. Not designed for repositories
             with very large worktree counts; v1's full-mode `--no-gh`
             run measured 14.94s across ~31 entries on the live 38-
             worktree checkout (the network round-trip `--no-gh` failed to
             suppress) - fixed in this round.
             Full mode IS now the mode an automatic call site uses (see the
             correction at the top of this section) - the backgrounded
             session-start reap invokes it unattended once per session
             start, so this per-entry cost is no longer merely a
             manual-invocation concern.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fnmatch
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared helpers: worktree_model.py (bin/tests/), imported by resolved path
# so a bare PATH-symlink invocation (~/.local/bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees ->
# repo bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees) still finds it - .resolve() follows the
# symlink back to the real bin/ directory, matching the pattern established
# in bin/ds-branch-prune and bin/ds-feedback.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
_BIN_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(_BIN_DIR / "tests"))

from worktree_model import (  # noqa: E402
    MERGE_EVIDENCE_ORDER,
    Disposition,
    DispositionFacts,
    WorktreeClass,
    classify_entry,
    disposition_for,
    parse_porcelain,
)

#: Every network-touching subprocess call in this file is bounded by this
#: timeout (round-2 Major 4) - `git ls-remote`, `gh auth status`, and
#: `gh pr list` all cross the network. A timeout degrades that evidence
#: leg to inconclusive, identically to a `gh` error - it can only ever
#: cause FEWER removals, never a false ELIGIBLE.
_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 8

#: Round-3 OPERATOR DECISION: the gitignored-content policy is a PROTECTED
#: DENYLIST by default, not a fail-safe allowlist. `git status --porcelain`
#: (no `--ignored` flag) never surfaces gitignored content, and a non-force
#: `git worktree remove` deletes it unconditionally regardless - proven
#: empirically in a scratch repo where a worktree holding only a gitignored
#: `.agentic/plan.md` reported a CLEAN `git status --porcelain` and was
#: silently destroyed. Round 2 shipped the opposite polarity (a fail-safe
#: ALLOWLIST - block unless the ignored path is a known-ephemeral
#: build/dependency artifact) and measured `removed=0` against the live
#: checkout: nearly every worktree here carries generated adapter output
#: (`.kimi/skills/*/`, etc.) that isn't a build artifact by name but also
#: isn't irreplaceable, so the allowlist blocked everything and the tool
#: reaped nothing - the ORIGINAL complaint this ticket exists to fix. The
#: operator explicitly traded a small unanticipated-authored-file risk
#: (an ignored file outside this protected set that turns out to matter,
#: which this denylist will not catch) for a tool that actually reaps by
#: default. The old fail-safe allowlist remains available via
#: `--strict-ignored` for an operator who wants the more conservative
#: (and, per round 2's own measurement, far less useful) behavior instead.
#: Single named constant, per the operator decision - do not scatter these
#: patterns elsewhere. `docs/planning/**` (gitignored plans) and
#: `.env*`/`*.local` (secrets/local config) are simple always-protected
#: prefixes/patterns. `.agentic/**` is handled separately, below, by
#: `_is_agentic_disposable` - round 4 inverted the polarity INSIDE
#: `.agentic/` specifically (see that function's docstring).
_PROTECTED_CONTENT_DIR_PREFIXES = ("docs/planning",)
_PROTECTED_CONTENT_BASENAME_PATTERNS = (
    ".env*",  # secrets
    "*.local",  # local config
)

#: Round-4 OPERATOR DECISION (correcting round 3): `.agentic/**` was meant
#: to protect AUTHORED work (plans, notes, decisions), not routine
#: telemetry - round 3's blanket `.agentic/**` protection swept in
#: `.agentic/events.jsonl` and friends, and because this repo dogfoods its
#: own methodology every worktree that has ever hosted an agent
#: accumulates that telemetry, making protection effectively universal
#: and measuring `removed=0` again. The rule INSIDE `.agentic/` is now
#: INVERTED relative to everywhere else: everything under `.agentic/` is
#: PROTECTED by default (fail-safe - an unanticipated new file blocks,
#: never silently vanishes) EXCEPT this named disposable set. Do not
#: scatter these patterns elsewhere either - see `_is_agentic_disposable`.
_AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_DIR_NAMES = (
    "wrap",
    "codex-prompt-generation",
    "hud",
    "cache",  # ordinary cache dirs
    "__pycache__",  # ordinary cache dirs
    ".cache",  # ordinary cache dirs
)
_AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_BASENAME_PATTERNS = (
    "*.jsonl",  # events.jsonl and any other telemetry log
    "tracker-states.json",
    ".skill-candidate-tally.json",
    "worktree-cleanup-skips.jsonl",  # also covered by *.jsonl above; named explicitly per the operator's own list
)

#: Ephemeral/regenerable allowlist - the round-2 default, kept reachable
#: via `--strict-ignored` (round-3). Under `--strict-ignored`, an ignored
#: path blocks removal UNLESS it is covered here; everything else ignored
#: (including generated adapter output like `.kimi/`, `.codex/`,
#: `.claude/skills/`) is treated as potentially irreplaceable and blocks.
_IGNORED_ALLOWLIST_DIR_NAMES = (
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "build",
    ".venv",
    "__pycache__",
    ".next",
    "target",
    "coverage",
)
_IGNORED_ALLOWLIST_PATTERNS = (".DS_Store", "*.log")

#: Round-6 correction (Skeptic Major 2): the EXPLICIT whitelist of
#: `Disposition` values `--archive-unproven` is permitted to archive. Named
#: and closed by construction - a disposition NOT on this set is excluded
#: by default, including any added to `worktree_model.py` later. Round 5's
#: bug was filtering on the `SKIP_UNPROVEN` outcome bucket alone (see
#: `evaluate_entry`), which silently swept in `SKIP_PR_OPEN` (a hard safety
#: override - an OPEN PR is affirmative evidence of live work, never
#: "unresolved branch content") and `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR` (a transient
#: network failure, not proof of anything). Only these two dispositions
#: mean "the branch content itself is the ONLY unresolved question":
#: `SKIP_NOT_PUSHED` (branch never reached origin at all) and
#: `SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR` (every evidence source was inconclusive - no PR,
#: no merge evidence, no subsumption proof) - PROVIDED the PR-state query
#: that helped produce that disposition actually succeeded (see
#: `evaluate_entry`'s gate 9 / `SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR`: a per-entry `gh pr
#: list` failure reclassifies the entry to a bucket that is never
#: `SKIP_UNPROVEN` at all, so this whitelist never even sees it). See the
#: module docstring's "Archiving unproven branches" section for the full
#: rationale, including why `--archive-unproven` additionally refuses to
#: run in degraded gh mode rather than relying on this whitelist alone.
_ARCHIVABLE_UNPROVEN_DISPOSITIONS = frozenset(
    {
        Disposition.SKIP_NOT_PUSHED.value,
        Disposition.SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR.value,
    }
)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# git/gh plumbing
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _run(
    args: List[str], cwd: Optional[str] = None, timeout: Optional[float] = None
) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    try:
        return subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        # Synthetic failure result - callers treat any nonzero returncode as
        # "could not determine", never as proof of anything. rc=124 mirrors
        # the shell convention for a timed-out command.
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(args, returncode=124, stdout="", stderr=f"timeout after {timeout}s")


#: Timeout (seconds) for `_measure_size_kb`'s `du -sk` call, opt-in via
#: `--measure-size` only (see that flag's own docstring for why it must
#: never be passed by an automatic caller). An earlier design used
#: `timeout 10` and a reviewer showed that is self-defeating: `du -sk`
#: cost scales with inode count more than raw bytes, and the trees this
#: feature most needs to measure accurately are the LARGEST ones - a
#: `SKIP_DIRTY` worktree tree can run to double-digit GB (one repo's
#: `.claude/worktrees` measured at 23 GB, another at 18 GB), and a 10s
#: bound would systematically time out on exactly the entries whose size
#: matters most, always reporting them `size_unknown` rather than
#: measured. Measured reference points on this machine: a 66 MB /
#: 1727-inode tree took 0.37s warm; a ~6.8 GB tree with four
#: `node_modules` directories took 0.798s warm - both suggest even a
#: 23 GB tree should complete in well under a minute on local disk. Since
#: this flag is opt-in and operator-invoked (never an automatic caller's
#: cost profile), there is no pressure to shave the bound tighter: 300s
#: (5 minutes) gives generous headroom for a cold cache, a slower
#: filesystem, or a network-backed mount, while still eventually giving
#: up rather than hanging forever.
_MEASURE_SIZE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 300.0


def _measure_size_kb(path: str, timeout: float = _MEASURE_SIZE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS) -> Optional[int]:
    """Wraps `du -sk <path>` to measure a worktree's on-disk size in KB.
    Returns `None` on ANY failure - missing path, non-numeric/empty
    output, or the timeout above - NEVER `0`. A fabricated `0` would
    silently understate a byte-reporting total (the exact failure class
    `--measure-size` exists to prevent) and would be indistinguishable
    from a genuinely-empty directory. Callers must exclude a `None`
    result from any sum and count it via `size_unknown_count` instead.

    Measures the working-tree checkout only - never the shared `.git`
    object store, which every worktree of a repo shares (removing a
    worktree never frees any of it; only `git gc` after a branch delete
    can, and only for objects that become unreachable).
    """
    proc = _run(["du", "-sk", path], timeout=timeout)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    first_field = proc.stdout.split(None, 1)[0] if proc.stdout.strip() else ""
    if not first_field.isdigit():
        return None
    return int(first_field)


def _resolve_repo(path: str) -> str:
    p = Path(path).expanduser()
    if not p.is_dir():
        raise ValueError(f"not a directory: {path}")
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", str(p), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise ValueError(f"not a git repository: {path}")
    return proc.stdout.strip()


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base-branch resolution (round-4 rework, DS-cleanup-worktrees). Replaces
# content/commands/ds-cleanup-worktrees.md's former Step 2 hand-rolled
# grep/awk/sed text-extraction pipeline, which produced a fresh defect in
# three consecutive review rounds (matched prose, mishandled quotes,
# doubled origin/ prefix, a fenced-example `head -1` win; trailing
# whitespace surviving normalization; a fence-stripper that missed indented
# and ~~~ fences; unstripped single quotes; end-of-line anchoring that
# rejected a trailing comment) - each fix was an input-variant patch on the
# last, so this round moves resolution into tested Python instead.
#
# Resolution order, first candidate that resolves AND validates wins:
#   a. explicit --base CLI argument, if the operator passed one - used
#      VERBATIM, no validation/fallthrough (preserves this tool's
#      pre-existing precedence for an operator override; a bad explicit
#      value is not silently second-guessed).
#   b. a `BASE_BRANCH:` declaration in the repo's AGENTS.md - AUTHORITATIVE,
#      not merely first-ranked: if declared but its `origin/<name>` ref does
#      not validate, resolution fails outright (source "unresolved") rather
#      than falling through to tier c or below. conventions.md's canonical
#      order says the declaration "wins. Highest priority," and a silent
#      substitution of a different (possibly perfectly valid) base is a
#      more dangerous failure than a skipped run - the substituted base
#      being valid is exactly what would let a wrongful reap slip through
#      undetected. Only tiers c-f below have a same-tier-to-next-tier
#      fallthrough on validation failure.
#   c. `git symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`.
#   d. a local `develop` branch.
#   e. a local `development` branch.
#   f. `main`, then `master`.
# Tiers b-f each resolve to an `origin/<name>` ref (mirroring the shell
# pipeline this replaces, which always compared against the REMOTE branch)
# and are each validated with `git rev-parse --verify` before use. Among
# tiers c-f, a resolved-but-nonexistent candidate is reported via a
# diagnostic and the next candidate is tried, never a hard failure on the
# first miss (tier b is the sole exception - see above). Only when every
# candidate fails does resolution fail, naming every candidate tried.
#
# This order deliberately differs from content/rules/conventions.md's
# canonical §Base branch resolution in three ways, all because this tool
# runs non-interactively with no operator to prompt:
#   1. it inserts an extra tier (c, origin/HEAD) between the AGENTS.md
#      declaration and the local develop/development tiers - the
#      strongest automatic signal of a repo's actual configured default
#      branch when AGENTS.md declares nothing, ahead of a local
#      develop/development branch that could be an unrelated leftover
#      branch a contributor happened to create locally;
#   2. conventions.md's final step is an interactive prompt (offering
#      main, falling back to master, with an explicit decline path) - this
#      tool has no operator to prompt, so it resolves main (falling back
#      to master) directly instead of stopping to ask;
#   3. a declared-but-unresolvable AGENTS.md base fails resolution outright
#      instead of falling through to a lower tier - conventions.md's prose
#      does not itself specify fallthrough-on-declared-but-unresolvable
#      behavior, since its resolution is interactive; this tool makes the
#      choice explicit rather than picking silent substitution by default.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

#: Matches a `BASE_BRANCH:` declaration line, tolerating an optional
#: leading backtick before the label (a whole-phrase inline-code span, a
#: common markdown convention), an optional matching quote/backtick pair
#: wrapping just the value, and an optional `refs/heads/`/`origin/`
#: prefix. The `trailer` group captures everything after the value so
#: `_ALLOWED_DECL_TRAILER_RE` below can distinguish a genuine declaration
#: from a prose sentence that merely MENTIONS `BASE_BRANCH:` (e.g.
#: "BASE_BRANCH: resolution rules.", the round-2 false-match defect).
_BASE_BRANCH_DECL_RE = re.compile(
    r"`?BASE_BRANCH:\s*"
    r"(?P<quote>[`\"']?)"
    r"(?P<value>(?:refs/heads/|origin/)?[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_./-]*)"
    r"(?P=quote)"
    r"`?"  # closes an optional whole-phrase-wrapping leading backtick above
    r"(?P<trailer>.*)$"
)

#: A declaration's trailer is allowed to be empty, a bare closing period,
#: and/or a `#`- or `//`-prefixed trailing comment - anything else (extra
#: prose words) means the whole line was a mention, not a declaration.
_ALLOWED_DECL_TRAILER_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(\.\s*)?(#.*|//.*)?\s*$")


def _strip_fenced_and_indented_lines(text: str) -> List[str]:
    """Blank out fenced (``` / ~~~) and 4-space/tab-indented code regions
    so a `BASE_BRANCH:` mention inside an illustrative example can never
    match - only a genuine declaration in prose can. Line COUNT and ORDER
    are preserved (fenced/indented lines become empty strings, never
    removed), so a real declaration appearing AFTER a fenced example in
    the same file is still reachable on its own line."""
    lines = text.splitlines()
    out: List[str] = []
    in_fence = False
    for line in lines:
        stripped = line.lstrip(" \t")
        if stripped.startswith("```") or stripped.startswith("~~~"):
            in_fence = not in_fence
            out.append("")
            continue
        if in_fence:
            out.append("")
            continue
        if line[:4] == "    " or line[:1] == "\t":
            out.append("")
            continue
        out.append(line)
    return out


def _parse_base_branch_declaration(agents_md_text: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """Extract a `BASE_BRANCH:` declaration's value from AGENTS.md prose.
    Returns None when no genuine declaration is found (no matching line,
    every match is inside a fenced/indented example, or every match is a
    prose mention that fails the trailer check)."""
    for line in _strip_fenced_and_indented_lines(agents_md_text):
        m = _BASE_BRANCH_DECL_RE.search(line)
        if not m:
            continue
        if not _ALLOWED_DECL_TRAILER_RE.match(m.group("trailer")):
            continue
        value = m.group("value")
        if value.startswith("refs/heads/"):
            value = value[len("refs/heads/"):]
        elif value.startswith("origin/"):
            value = value[len("origin/"):]
        # Branch names never legitimately end in "." (git rejects them,
        # git-check-ref-format(1)) - the value char class allows "." mid-
        # token (e.g. "release-1.2") so a trailing sentence period gets
        # greedily consumed into the value at true end-of-line; strip
        # exactly one.
        if len(value) > 1 and value.endswith("."):
            value = value[:-1]
        return value
    return None


def _read_agents_md(repo: str) -> Optional[str]:
    try:
        return (Path(repo) / "AGENTS.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return None


def _local_branch_exists(repo: str, name: str) -> bool:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "show-ref", "--verify", "--quiet", f"refs/heads/{name}"])
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _origin_head_branch(repo: str) -> Optional[str]:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    ref = proc.stdout.strip()
    prefix = "refs/remotes/origin/"
    if ref.startswith(prefix) and len(ref) > len(prefix):
        return ref[len(prefix):]
    return None


def _ref_exists(repo: str, ref: str) -> bool:
    # Checks the LOCAL remote-tracking ref (e.g. `origin/main`), not the
    # remote itself - no network call is made here. A branch that genuinely
    # exists on origin but whose local remote-tracking ref is stale or was
    # never fetched will report as missing; `git fetch origin` is the fix.
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref])
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _has_any_remote(repo: str) -> bool:
    """True when `repo` has at least one git remote configured (`git
    remote`, no network call). Round-N Major 1 fix: every automatic base
    candidate is an `origin/<name>` ref, so a repo with zero remotes can
    never resolve one regardless of which branch name is tried - this is
    the ACTUAL cause of every live `resolve_base_branch` failure measured
    under `--multi-repo --report --count-only` (11 of 11 `skipped-base-
    unresolved` repos, verified: zero non-root worktrees on 10 of the 11,
    none an AGENTS.md `BASE_BRANCH:` failure). Distinguishing this case
    lets `resolve_base_branch` give advice that is actually actionable for
    it, instead of the generic "declare BASE_BRANCH or pass --base" text,
    which is a dead end here: declaring BASE_BRANCH still resolves against
    `origin/<name>` and cannot help, and `--base` is a hard usage error
    under `--multi-repo` (see `_validate_args`)."""
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "remote"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return False
    return bool(proc.stdout.strip())


def resolve_base_branch(
    repo: str, explicit_base: Optional[str], multi_repo: bool = False
) -> Tuple[Optional[str], str, List[str]]:
    """Resolve the --base ref per the tier order documented in the module
    comment above. Returns (resolved_ref, source_tier, diagnostics):
    `resolved_ref` is None either when an AGENTS.md `BASE_BRANCH:`
    declaration is present but its ref fails validation (declaration is
    authoritative, never falls through to a lower tier - see module
    comment), or when every tier-c-through-f automatic candidate failed
    validation (`explicit_base` given always resolves, tier "explicit");
    `source_tier` is one of "explicit", "agents-md", "origin-head",
    "local-develop", "local-development", "main-fallback",
    "master-fallback", or "unresolved" when resolved_ref is None;
    `diagnostics` is zero or more human-readable lines - one per candidate
    that resolved to a value but failed `git rev-parse --verify` against
    the LOCAL remote-tracking ref (e.g. `origin/main`), not the remote
    itself - a ref that is stale or was never fetched reads as missing even
    when the branch genuinely exists on origin (`git fetch origin` fixes
    this) - plus (only when every candidate failed) a final line naming
    every candidate tried. `multi_repo` (round-N Major 1) is used ONLY to
    keep the final "every candidate failed" diagnostic's remediation
    advice mode-aware: suggesting `--base` is useless (a hard usage error,
    exit 2) when this resolution is happening under `--multi-repo`, so
    that branch of the message is omitted there.
    """
    diagnostics: List[str] = []

    if explicit_base is not None:
        return explicit_base, "explicit", diagnostics

    # A `BASE_BRANCH:` declaration in AGENTS.md is authoritative, not just a
    # ranked candidate - conventions.md's canonical resolution order says
    # the declaration "wins. Highest priority." A declared-but-unresolvable
    # value must never silently fall through to origin/HEAD, a local
    # develop/development branch, or main/master: those could easily be
    # VALID branches that resolve cleanly, so a fallthrough would reap
    # against a base the operator never declared, with no error surfaced -
    # the dangerous case is exactly the one where the substituted base is
    # itself valid. Fail the whole resolution here (never a hard crash;
    # `resolved_ref is None` already fails safe at the caller, which skips
    # the reap for this run without exiting nonzero).
    agents_md_text = _read_agents_md(repo)
    if agents_md_text is not None:
        declared = _parse_base_branch_declaration(agents_md_text)
        if declared:
            ref = f"origin/{declared}"
            if _ref_exists(repo, ref):
                return ref, "agents-md", diagnostics
            diagnostics.append(
                f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: declared base candidate '{ref}' (source: agents-md) "
                "does not exist as a local remote-tracking ref - AGENTS.md's BASE_BRANCH "
                "declaration is authoritative and is never silently overridden by a "
                "different base; resolution fails rather than falling through. If the "
                "branch genuinely exists on origin, this ref may simply be stale or "
                "unfetched - try `git fetch origin` first. Otherwise, fix the branch name "
                "in AGENTS.md's BASE_BRANCH declaration, or pass --base explicitly to "
                "override the declaration for this run."
            )
            return None, "unresolved", diagnostics

    candidates: List[Tuple[str, str]] = []

    origin_head = _origin_head_branch(repo)
    if origin_head:
        candidates.append((f"origin/{origin_head}", "origin-head"))

    if _local_branch_exists(repo, "develop"):
        candidates.append(("origin/develop", "local-develop"))
    if _local_branch_exists(repo, "development"):
        candidates.append(("origin/development", "local-development"))

    candidates.append(("origin/main", "main-fallback"))
    candidates.append(("origin/master", "master-fallback"))

    for ref, source in candidates:
        if _ref_exists(repo, ref):
            return ref, source, diagnostics
        diagnostics.append(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: base candidate '{ref}' (source: {source}) does not exist "
            "on origin - trying the next candidate."
        )

    tried = ", ".join(f"{ref} ({source})" for ref, source in candidates)

    # Round-N Major 1 fix: every candidate above is an `origin/<name>` ref,
    # so a repo with zero git remotes can never resolve ANY of them - this
    # is the actual, measured cause of every live failure (see
    # `_has_any_remote`'s docstring). Name that cause specifically and give
    # mode-aware advice, instead of the generic "declare BASE_BRANCH or
    # pass --base" text, which is a dead end here: a BASE_BRANCH
    # declaration still resolves against `origin/<name>` and cannot help,
    # and suggesting `--base` under `--multi-repo` would recommend a flag
    # that is a hard usage error there (see `_validate_args`).
    if not _has_any_remote(repo):
        remediation = (
            "This repo has no git remotes configured (`git remote` returned nothing), so no "
            "`origin/<branch>` ref can ever resolve here regardless of which branch name is "
            "tried - declaring BASE_BRANCH in AGENTS.md will not help either, since it also "
            "resolves against `origin/<name>`. Add a remote (`git remote add origin <url>`) "
            "and fetch, or skip this repo."
        )
        if not multi_repo:
            remediation += (
                " Alternatively, pass --base <ref> explicitly - it bypasses remote resolution "
                "entirely and is accepted verbatim."
            )
        diagnostics.append(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: could not resolve a base branch - no git remotes configured. "
            f"Tried: {tried}. {remediation}"
        )
    else:
        advice = "Declare BASE_BRANCH in AGENTS.md"
        advice += ", or pass --base explicitly." if not multi_repo else " for this repo."
        diagnostics.append(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: could not resolve a base branch - every candidate failed. "
            f"Tried: {tried}. {advice}"
        )
    return None, "unresolved", diagnostics


def _worktree_list(repo: str) -> str:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"git worktree list failed: {proc.stderr.strip()}")
    return proc.stdout


def _is_allowlisted_ignored_path(rel_path: str) -> bool:
    """`--strict-ignored` mode (round-3): True when `rel_path` is covered
    by the ephemeral/regenerable allowlist - i.e. safe to ignore."""
    parts = rel_path.strip("/").split("/")
    if any(part in _IGNORED_ALLOWLIST_DIR_NAMES for part in parts):
        return True
    basename = parts[-1] if parts else rel_path
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(basename, pattern) for pattern in _IGNORED_ALLOWLIST_PATTERNS)


def _is_agentic_disposable(sub_parts: List[str]) -> bool:
    """Round 4: `sub_parts` is a path INSIDE `.agentic/`, given as its
    segments AFTER the leading `.agentic` segment (e.g. `.agentic/wrap/lock`
    -> `["wrap", "lock"]`). Returns True only when it is on the named
    disposable set (`_AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_DIR_NAMES` /
    `_AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_BASENAME_PATTERNS`) - the INVERTED default for
    everywhere else in `_is_protected_ignored_path`: inside `.agentic/`,
    NOT being disposable means PROTECTED (fail-safe - an unanticipated new
    file under `.agentic/` blocks removal, it does not silently vanish).
    A bare/collapsed `.agentic/` entry (whole directory ignored as one
    unit, `sub_parts == []`) is never disposable - it could contain a
    protected file mixed in and there is no way to tell from a collapsed
    entry alone, so it fails closed as protected.
    """
    if not sub_parts:
        return False
    if sub_parts[0] in _AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_DIR_NAMES:
        return True
    basename = sub_parts[-1]
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(basename, pattern) for pattern in _AGENTIC_DISPOSABLE_BASENAME_PATTERNS)


def _is_protected_ignored_path(rel_path: str) -> bool:
    """Default mode (round-3 operator decision, round-4 correction inside
    `.agentic/`): True when `rel_path` blocks removal, False when it is
    disposable.

    `.agentic/**` is handled FIRST and separately, with INVERTED polarity
    from the rest of this function (see `_is_agentic_disposable`'s
    docstring): protected by default, disposable only for the named set.
    Everywhere else (`docs/planning/**`, `.env*`, `*.local`), the round-3
    polarity is unchanged: protected ONLY when it matches one of those
    patterns; everything else is disposable.

    A directory-form ignore entry (e.g. `git status --ignored=matching`
    reporting a collapsed `docs/planning/` when the whole directory is
    ignored, rather than descending into it) is matched as a PREFIX via
    the leading path segments, not only as an exact filename.
    """
    normalized = rel_path.strip("/")
    parts = normalized.split("/")

    if parts[0] == ".agentic":
        return not _is_agentic_disposable(parts[1:])

    for prefix in _PROTECTED_CONTENT_DIR_PREFIXES:
        prefix_parts = prefix.split("/")
        if parts[: len(prefix_parts)] == prefix_parts:
            return True
    basename = parts[-1] if parts else normalized
    return any(fnmatch.fnmatch(basename, pattern) for pattern in _PROTECTED_CONTENT_BASENAME_PATTERNS)


def _git_status_and_ignored(worktree_path: str, strict_ignored: bool) -> Tuple[str, List[str]]:
    """Single combined `git status --porcelain --ignored=matching` call.
    Returns (dirty_status, offending_ignored_paths). dirty_status is
    "clean" | "dirty" - a git error fails CLOSED as "dirty" (never a
    silent "clean" on an indeterminate read). `offending_ignored_paths` is
    the list of ignored relative paths that BLOCK removal under the active
    mode: everything NOT on the ephemeral allowlist when `strict_ignored`
    is True (round-2 behavior), or everything matching the protected
    denylist when `strict_ignored` is False (round-3 default - see
    `_is_protected_ignored_path`'s docstring and the operator-decision
    comment on `_PROTECTED_CONTENT_DIR_PREFIXES` above).
    """
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", worktree_path, "status", "--porcelain", "--ignored=matching"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return "dirty", []
    offenders: List[str] = []
    has_dirty_line = False
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            continue
        if line.startswith("!! "):
            rel = line[3:]
            blocked = (not _is_allowlisted_ignored_path(rel)) if strict_ignored else _is_protected_ignored_path(rel)
            if blocked:
                offenders.append(rel)
        else:
            has_dirty_line = True
    return ("dirty" if has_dirty_line else "clean"), offenders


def _ls_remote_status(repo: str, branch: str) -> str:
    """Returns "pushed" | "not_pushed" | "error"."""
    proc = _run(
        ["git", "-C", repo, "ls-remote", "--exit-code", "--heads", "origin", branch],
        timeout=_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
    )
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return "pushed"
    if proc.returncode == 2:
        return "not_pushed"
    return "error"


def _is_ancestor(repo: str, ancestor: str, descendant: str) -> bool:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", ancestor, descendant])
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _compute_origin_reachable(repo: str, head_sha: Optional[str]) -> str:
    """DS-196: `"reachable"` | `"unreachable"` | `"not_checked"`. LOCAL-ONLY
    (no network call) - `git -C repo branch -r --contains <sha> --list
    'origin/*'` lists every `origin/*` remote-tracking ref that contains
    `head_sha` in its history. Nonempty stdout means at least one such ref
    exists (not necessarily the base branch - any `origin/*` ref counts,
    which is what makes this evidence source useful for a squash-merged
    per-unit fan-out branch that was pushed and later fast-forwarded/
    replaced upstream but still has a surviving remote-tracking ref).
    Nonzero exit or a missing/empty `head_sha` fails CLOSED to
    `"not_checked"` - never `"unreachable"` on an indeterminate read, since
    this tool's callers treat `"not_checked"` as inconclusive (never a
    green light) while `"unreachable"` is an affirmative negative result
    from a query that DID run successfully. Populates `facts.
    origin_reachable` ONLY - never `facts.head_reachable`, which remains
    unrelated, dead code (see the module docstring's Removal predicate
    gate 4a note and `worktree_model.DispositionFacts`'s own docstring).

    Depends on stale remote-tracking refs surviving locally - see the
    module docstring's `fetch.prune` caveat.
    """
    if not head_sha:
        return "not_checked"
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "branch", "-r", "--contains", head_sha, "--list", "origin/*"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return "not_checked"
    return "reachable" if proc.stdout.strip() else "unreachable"


def _gh_available(repo: str, no_gh: bool) -> bool:
    if no_gh or shutil.which("gh") is None:
        return False
    proc = _run(["gh", "auth", "status"], cwd=repo, timeout=_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _pr_state(repo: str, branch: str) -> Tuple[str, bool]:
    """Returns `(state, query_errored)`.

    `state` is one of `"OPEN"` | `"MERGED"` | `"CLOSED"` | `"NONE"` |
    `"not_checked"` - the same vocabulary `DispositionFacts.pr_state`
    already documents. `query_errored` is True ONLY when the query itself
    could not be answered (nonzero exit, unparseable/unexpected JSON, or a
    network timeout) - this is a DISTINCT fact from `state == "NONE"` (a
    genuine, successfully-confirmed absence of any PR for this branch) and
    callers MUST NEVER collapse the two, or a transient `gh` failure
    (rate limit, auth hiccup, network blip) on a branch that actually has
    an OPEN PR becomes indistinguishable from a branch that was never
    opened at all - the exact ambiguity that let `--archive-unproven`
    silently archive-and-remove a worktree behind a live PR (see the
    module docstring's "Archiving unproven branches" section).

    Runs the LITERAL argv `gh pr list --head <branch> --state all --json
    number,state` (no `--limit`) rather than `gh pr view <branch>` - `gh pr
    view` exits nonzero IDENTICALLY for "no PR exists for this branch" and
    for a genuine query failure, which is exactly the ambiguity this
    function exists to resolve. `gh pr list` exits 0 and returns `[]` on a
    genuine no-PR branch, so any nonzero exit or unparseable/malformed JSON
    here is unambiguous evidence of a QUERY failure, never proof of
    absence. `--state all` is required - the command's own default is
    open-only, which would silently misreport a MERGED/CLOSED PR as "no
    PR" - but `--state all` alone does NOT prevent misreporting a
    multi-PR head branch: a branch can carry more than one PR over its
    lifetime (e.g. closed-then-reopened-as-new, or a merged PR followed by
    a fresh one), and `gh pr list` orders results CREATED_AT DESC with no
    OPEN-first preference. A round-N Major fix removed a `--limit 1` that
    truncated to the single newest row and picked it by construction,
    which silently masked a live OPEN PR behind a more recent CLOSED or
    MERGED one - verified live against this repo (`gh pr list --state all
    --limit 5 --json number,state,createdAt` returned strict newest-first
    order spanning OPEN/MERGED/CLOSED). The fix below fetches every row
    (no `--limit`) and explicitly prefers OPEN over recency - see the
    selection logic itself.
    """
    proc = _run(
        ["gh", "pr", "list", "--head", branch, "--state", "all", "--json", "number,state"],
        cwd=repo,
        timeout=_NETWORK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
    )
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return "not_checked", True
    try:
        rows = json.loads(proc.stdout)
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return "not_checked", True
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        return "not_checked", True
    if not rows:
        return "NONE", False
    # Select by SAFETY, not recency: if ANY row is OPEN, that is the
    # disposition-relevant fact regardless of how many other PRs (older or
    # newer) also exist for this head branch - an OPEN PR is a hard safety
    # override (see Disposition.SKIP_PR_OPEN) and must never be masked by a
    # more recent CLOSED/MERGED row. Only fall back to the first row
    # (`gh pr list`'s own CREATED_AT DESC order) when no row is OPEN - do
    # NOT silently restore recency-only selection here on a later refactor.
    states: List[str] = []
    for row in rows:
        state = row.get("state") if isinstance(row, dict) else None
        if not state or not isinstance(state, str):
            return "not_checked", True
        states.append(state.upper())
    if "OPEN" in states:
        return "OPEN", False
    return states[0], False


def _is_self_worktree(entry_path: str) -> bool:
    """True when the CURRENT PROCESS is running from inside `entry_path`
    (round-2 Major 2a) - compared via `Path.cwd()`, resolved, against the
    entry's own resolved path. A v1 dry run flagged this tool's own live
    worktree as REMOVE; this is the absolute floor that makes that
    structurally impossible, independent of lock state.
    """
    try:
        cwd = Path.cwd().resolve()
        wt = Path(entry_path).resolve()
    except OSError:
        return False
    return cwd == wt or wt in cwd.parents


def _worktree_age_hours(entry_path: str) -> Optional[float]:
    """Hours since `entry_path`'s own mtime, or None if it cannot be
    stat'd (treated as "too young" by the caller - fail closed, never
    remove on an indeterminate age).
    """
    try:
        mtime = Path(entry_path).stat().st_mtime
    except OSError:
        return None
    return (time.time() - mtime) / 3600.0


#: DS-196: directories pruned from `_worktree_activity_hours`'s recursive
#: walk - trades completeness for cost (see the module docstring's
#: activity-scan trade-off note). A worktree where ONLY a build/test run
#: touched files inside one of these (all-gitignored) dirs finds no file
#: outside the pruned set, so `_worktree_activity_hours` returns `None` -
#: the OPPOSITE of "reads as idle": the caller's `activity_hours is None`
#: branch fails CLOSED (SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY), so this case is protected
#: from removal despite the recent tool activity, not eligible for it.
#: Accepted anyway, since removal never destroys commits and the false
#: protection only costs a delayed reap, not a false removal.
_ACTIVITY_SCAN_PRUNE_DIRS = frozenset(
    {".git", "node_modules", ".venv", "dist", "build", ".next", "__pycache__"}
)


def _worktree_activity_hours(entry_path: str) -> Optional[float]:
    """DS-196: hours since the most recent mtime among FILES (never
    directories themselves) under `entry_path`, `os.walk`-pruning
    `_ACTIVITY_SCAN_PRUNE_DIRS`. Returns `None` when no file could be
    stat'd at all (e.g. the directory is empty or unreadable) - the
    caller treats `None` as "too recent to know" and fails CLOSED
    (never removed), mirroring `_worktree_age_hours`'s own None handling.
    """
    latest: Optional[float] = None
    try:
        for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(entry_path):
            dirnames[:] = [d for d in dirnames if d not in _ACTIVITY_SCAN_PRUNE_DIRS]
            for filename in filenames:
                try:
                    mtime = os.path.getmtime(os.path.join(dirpath, filename))
                except OSError:
                    continue
                if latest is None or mtime > latest:
                    latest = mtime
    except OSError:
        return None
    if latest is None:
        return None
    return (time.time() - latest) / 3600.0


_BRANCH_SAFE_CHARS_RE = re.compile(r"[^A-Za-z0-9._-]")


def _worktree_has_salvageable_telemetry(worktree_path: str) -> bool:
    """True when `<worktree_path>/.agentic/events.jsonl` exists and is
    non-empty - i.e. there is something for `_salvage_telemetry` to
    actually salvage. Used both by the real salvage path and by the
    --dry-run "would salvage" count, so the two can never disagree about
    what counts as salvageable.
    """
    src = Path(worktree_path) / ".agentic" / "events.jsonl"
    try:
        return src.is_file() and src.stat().st_size > 0
    except OSError:
        return False


def _salvage_telemetry(repo: str, worktree_path: str, branch: Optional[str]) -> Tuple[bool, str]:
    """Round-4 requirement: copies `<worktree_path>/.agentic/events.jsonl`
    into `<repo>/.agentic/reaped-telemetry/<branch>-<timestamp>.jsonl`
    BEFORE the worktree is removed - telemetry is disposable for the
    purpose of BLOCKING removal, but it is not worthless (ds-cost /
    retro read it), so it must survive the worktree's own deletion.

    Returns (ok, detail). `ok=True` covers two cases: (a) there was
    nothing to salvage (no `events.jsonl`, or it is empty) - a no-op,
    correctly not blocking removal; (b) the copy succeeded AND was
    verified to have actually landed non-empty at the destination.
    `ok=False` on ANY other failure (source unreadable, destination
    directory uncreatable, copy error, or post-copy verification
    failing) - the caller MUST NOT remove the worktree when `ok=False`;
    a failed salvage must never become a silent deletion of the telemetry
    it was trying to preserve.

    `.agentic/reaped-telemetry/` needs no new `.gitignore` carve-out: the
    existing `/.agentic/*` umbrella (content/.gitignore's own root-level
    entry, unchanged by this ticket) already covers any new top-level
    entry under `.agentic/`, including this one.
    """
    if not _worktree_has_salvageable_telemetry(worktree_path):
        return True, "nothing to salvage"

    src = Path(worktree_path) / ".agentic" / "events.jsonl"
    dest_dir = Path(repo) / ".agentic" / "reaped-telemetry"
    branch_safe = _BRANCH_SAFE_CHARS_RE.sub("_", branch) if branch else "unknown-branch"
    timestamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime())
    dest = dest_dir / f"{branch_safe}-{timestamp}.jsonl"

    try:
        dest_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(str(src), str(dest))
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, f"copy to {dest} failed: {exc}"

    # Verify the copy actually landed and is non-empty - never trust a
    # copy call's own success return alone (e.g. a race, a filesystem
    # quirk, or a partial write could still leave nothing usable behind).
    try:
        if not dest.is_file() or dest.stat().st_size == 0:
            return False, f"copy to {dest} did not verify (missing or empty)"
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, f"verification of {dest} failed: {exc}"

    return True, f"salvaged to {dest}"


def _salvage_and_remove(repo: str, worktree_path: str, branch: Optional[str]) -> Dict:
    """Round-6 requirement (Skeptic Major 1): the SINGLE salvage-then-remove
    sequence both the plain REMOVE loop and the `--archive-unproven`
    archive loop call - round 5 shipped the archive loop calling
    `git worktree remove` directly, bypassing `_salvage_telemetry`
    entirely, so an unproven worktree holding real telemetry was silently
    destroyed with `salvaged=0`. Factoring this into one function makes
    that divergence structurally impossible: neither loop may call
    `git worktree remove` on a REMOVE/ARCHIVED_AND_REMOVED-bound entry
    except through here.

    Returns a dict with keys:
      `salvage_failed` - True when `_salvage_telemetry` returned `ok=False`.
                          `removed` and `salvaged` are both False/irrelevant
                          when this is True - `git worktree remove` is
                          NEVER invoked when salvage fails (same guard
                          `_salvage_telemetry`'s own docstring requires).
      `salvaged`        - True when telemetry was actually copied (not
                          merely "nothing to salvage").
      `removed`         - True when `git worktree remove` succeeded.
      `detail`          - the salvage failure detail (when `salvage_failed`)
                          or the `git worktree remove` stderr (when
                          `not removed`), else "".
    """
    salvage_ok, salvage_detail = _salvage_telemetry(repo, worktree_path, branch)
    if not salvage_ok:
        return {"salvage_failed": True, "salvaged": False, "removed": False, "detail": salvage_detail}

    salvaged = salvage_detail.startswith("salvaged to ")
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "remove", worktree_path])
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return {"salvage_failed": False, "salvaged": salvaged, "removed": True, "detail": ""}
    return {"salvage_failed": False, "salvaged": salvaged, "removed": False, "detail": proc.stderr.strip()}


def _restore_command(bundle_path: str, branch: str) -> str:
    """The exact restore command for a bundle archived by
    `_archive_branch_bundle` below. Per this repo's own documented
    gotcha (AGENTS.md: "Brace any shell expansion followed by a literal
    `:` in a refspec"), the branch variable is braced in the refspec -
    an unbraced `$VAR:` is mangled by zsh.

    A compact bundle (see `_archive_branch_bundle`) records its
    exclusion boundary as a prerequisite commit, not as a permanent
    guarantee: `git bundle verify` at archive time only proves that
    commit exists in THIS repo's object store right now, not that it
    will remain reachable forever - a later force-push/rewrite-and-gc of
    the base branch could still invalidate an already-archived compact
    bundle. A full-history bundle (no exclusion applied) has no such
    dependency and is self-contained.
    """
    return f'git fetch {bundle_path} "refs/heads/{branch}:refs/heads/{branch}"'


def _archive_compaction_base(
    repo: str, resolved_base: str, base_source: str
) -> Optional[str]:
    """Round-N (DS-191 MAJOR-2 fix): decides which ref, if any, is safe to
    pass as `_archive_branch_bundle`'s `exclude_ref`.

    `resolve_base_branch` returns an explicit `--base` COMPLETELY
    UNVALIDATED (`if explicit_base is not None: return explicit_base,
    "explicit", diagnostics`) - nothing constrains it to any of the
    durable candidates the auto-resolution tier order trusts. Compacting
    against an arbitrary caller-supplied ref unconditionally creates a
    NEW permanent-loss path this repo's own bundle-then-delete precedent
    was supposed to avoid: demonstrated end-to-end, `--base
    origin/feat-x` produces a `compact=True` bundle whose sole
    prerequisite is the tip of `feat-x` - delete that branch
    (`git push origin --delete feat-x`) and gc, and the restore then
    fails irrecoverably (`error: Repository lacks these prerequisite
    commits`). A full-history bundle has no such dependency and is
    always safe to produce.

    When `base_source != "explicit"`, `resolved_base` is already one of
    the trusted auto-resolution tier candidates (origin/HEAD, a
    declared/develop/development branch, or the main/master fallback) -
    return it unconditionally, unchanged from pre-fix behavior.

    When `base_source == "explicit"`, compaction is allowed ONLY if the
    explicit ref happens to resolve to the SAME commit that this same
    tool's own auto-resolution (the identical tier order used when
    `--base` is omitted) would have picked for this repo right now. In
    that case the bundle's prerequisite commit is ALSO reachable via the
    durable auto-resolved ref (e.g. `origin/main`) - the explicit ref
    itself being deleted later does not orphan the bundle, since the
    prerequisite commit persists via the other ref. Any other explicit
    ref (one that verifies and even shares history with the branch, but
    resolves to a different commit than auto-resolution would pick) is
    NOT compacted - full-history only, with a NOTE explaining why (see
    the sole call site).

    Returns the ref to use as `exclude_ref`, or `None` to force a
    full-history bundle. Never raises - a `resolve_base_branch` internal
    failure or an unresolvable auto-candidate here degrades to `None`
    (full-history), the same fail-safe direction `_archive_branch_bundle`
    already takes for every other not-compact cause.
    """
    if base_source != "explicit":
        return resolved_base
    auto_resolved, _auto_source, _diagnostics = resolve_base_branch(repo, None)
    if auto_resolved is None:
        return None
    explicit_sha = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", resolved_base])
    auto_sha = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", auto_resolved])
    if (
        explicit_sha.returncode == 0
        and auto_sha.returncode == 0
        and explicit_sha.stdout.strip()
        and explicit_sha.stdout.strip() == auto_sha.stdout.strip()
    ):
        return auto_resolved
    return None


def _archive_branch_bundle(
    repo: str, branch: str, exclude_ref: Optional[str]
) -> Tuple[bool, str, Optional[str], bool]:
    """Round-5 requirement, DS-191 compaction rework: archives the FULL
    branch `branch` (every commit unique to it, not just its tip) into
    `<repo>/.agentic/worktree-archive/<branch>-<timestamp>.bundle` via
    `git bundle create`, then VERIFIES the bundle with `git bundle
    verify` BEFORE the caller may remove anything - same discipline as
    `_salvage_telemetry`'s own guard: this tool proves subsumption is
    unprovable (`SKIP_UNPROVEN`) for these branches, so the archive,
    combined with this repo's own retained base objects, is the only
    remaining copy of unpushed work once the worktree is gone. This tool
    archives and removes WORKTREES only - it never deletes the branch
    itself (`git branch -D` is deliberately not called here); reclaiming
    the archived branch's own local ref remains `bin/ds-branch-prune`'s
    job, same as it already is for every other worktree-removal path in
    this tool (that tool does not itself bundle branches - see the
    module docstring's DS-153 correction).

    `exclude_ref` is the caller's already-resolved base ref (e.g.
    `resolve_base_branch`'s `resolved_ref`, "origin/main") - a ref this
    repo's own gh-pr-merge --delete-branch convention never routinely
    deletes (unlike a feature branch's own origin/* ref). When objects
    reachable from `exclude_ref` are excluded from the bundle, the
    result is a small, compact bundle carrying only the branch's unique
    commits, plus a recorded prerequisite naming the excluded tip -
    restoring it requires that prerequisite commit to still be present
    in this repo's object store (see `_restore_command`'s docstring).

    UNREACHABLE-AT-PRODUCTION-CALL-SITE NOTE: because the caller passes
    the SAME ref used for merge-evidence ancestor testing, any branch
    fully contained in `exclude_ref` (`unique_count == 0` below) already
    resolved to ELIGIBLE->REMOVE before the archive loop runs, and never
    reaches this function through the real CLI - the `unique_count == 0`
    fallback below is DEFENSIVE-ONLY, exercised only by a direct
    unit-level call that bypasses disposition routing.

    `exclude_ref is None` is NO LONGER dead at the production call site
    (round-N, DS-191 MAJOR-2 fix): the caller now routes `resolved_base`
    through `_archive_compaction_base` first, which returns `None`
    whenever an explicit `--base` does not resolve to the same commit
    this tool's own auto-resolution would have picked - see that
    function's docstring for why. That is a REAL, reachable production
    path, not merely a defensive one.

    Before creating the bundle, measures `unique_count = git rev-list
    --count <branch> --not <exclude_ref>` and `total_count = git
    rev-list --count <branch>`. Exclusion (`--not <exclude_ref>`) is
    applied ONLY when `0 < unique_count < total_count` - passing `--not`
    when `unique_count == 0` would make `git bundle create` fail with
    `fatal: Refusing to create empty bundle` (rc=128, measured). A
    TOCTOU race is possible: a concurrent `git fetch` can advance
    `exclude_ref` past the branch tip between the `rev-list --count`
    measurement above and the actual `bundle create` call, reproducing
    the same rc=128 failure despite the pre-check having passed. This is
    handled, not merely documented: on that EXACT failure signature
    (`--not` was used AND rc==128 AND stderr contains "Refusing to
    create empty bundle"), this function retries ONCE with the same argv
    minus `--not` (guaranteed non-empty - a positive branch rev is never
    empty) and reports `compact=False` for that retry. This preserves
    the invariant that a compaction attempt never turns a working
    archive into a stuck worktree, including under this race.

    Returns (ok, detail, bundle_path, compact). `ok=False` on ANY
    failure (archive directory uncreatable, `git bundle create` error
    even after the retry above, a missing/empty bundle file after
    create, or `git bundle verify` failing) - the caller MUST NOT remove
    the worktree when `ok=False`; a failed archive must never become a
    silent deletion of the only copy of unproven work. `bundle_path` and
    `compact` are `None`/`False` whenever `ok=False`. `compact` reflects
    the pre-create measurement (`0 < unique_count < total_count`) ONLY
    when `ok=True`, and is `False` on both the retry path above and
    every failure return. `detail` is always `"archived and verified"`
    on success - the compact/non-compact fact is carried by `compact`
    alone, printed by the caller.

    `.agentic/worktree-archive/` needs no new `.gitignore` carve-out for
    the same reason `.agentic/reaped-telemetry/` does not (see
    `_salvage_telemetry`'s docstring): the existing `/.agentic/*`
    umbrella already covers any new top-level entry under `.agentic/`.
    This directory grows unbounded - pruning it is the operator's own
    responsibility, exactly like `.agentic/branch-archive/`
    (`bin/ds-branch-prune`'s own precedent for this exact pattern).
    """
    archive_dir = Path(repo) / ".agentic" / "worktree-archive"
    branch_safe = _BRANCH_SAFE_CHARS_RE.sub("_", branch)
    timestamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime())
    bundle_path = archive_dir / f"{branch_safe}-{timestamp}.bundle"

    try:
        archive_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, f"could not create archive directory {archive_dir}: {exc}", None, False

    compact = False
    used_exclusion = False
    argv = ["git", "-C", repo, "bundle", "create", str(bundle_path), branch]
    if exclude_ref:
        exclude_verify = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", exclude_ref])
        if exclude_verify.returncode == 0:
            unique_proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-list", "--count", branch, "--not", exclude_ref])
            total_proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-list", "--count", branch])
            if unique_proc.returncode == 0 and total_proc.returncode == 0:
                try:
                    unique_count = int(unique_proc.stdout.strip())
                    total_count = int(total_proc.stdout.strip())
                except ValueError:
                    unique_count = total_count = None
                if unique_count is not None and total_count is not None and 0 < unique_count < total_count:
                    argv += ["--not", exclude_ref]
                    compact = True
                    used_exclusion = True

    proc = _run(argv)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        if used_exclusion and proc.returncode == 128 and "Refusing to create empty bundle" in proc.stderr:
            # TOCTOU: exclude_ref advanced past the branch tip between the
            # rev-list measurement above and this call - retry once without
            # exclusion (always non-empty), never leave the worktree stuck.
            compact = False
            retry_argv = ["git", "-C", repo, "bundle", "create", str(bundle_path), branch]
            proc = _run(retry_argv)
            if proc.returncode != 0:
                return False, f"git bundle create failed (after retry): {proc.stderr.strip()}", None, False
        else:
            return False, f"git bundle create failed: {proc.stderr.strip()}", None, False

    try:
        if not bundle_path.is_file() or bundle_path.stat().st_size == 0:
            return False, f"bundle {bundle_path} missing or empty after create", None, False
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, f"could not stat {bundle_path}: {exc}", None, False

    verify_proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "bundle", "verify", str(bundle_path)])
    if verify_proc.returncode != 0:
        return False, f"git bundle verify failed: {verify_proc.stderr.strip()}", None, False

    return True, "archived and verified", str(bundle_path), compact


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-entry evidence + evaluation (round-2: delegates the LOCKED/DIRTY/
# branch-evidence decision entirely to worktree_model.disposition_for -
# see the module docstring's Removal predicate section)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _build_facts(
    repo: str,
    entry,
    dirty_status: str,
    base_ref: str,
    gh_ok: bool,
    allow_network: bool,
) -> Tuple[DispositionFacts, bool]:
    """Returns `(facts, pr_query_errored)`. `pr_query_errored` is True only
    when `_pr_state` could not answer the PR-state query at all - see that
    function's own docstring for why this must never be folded into
    `facts.pr_state == "NONE"` (the shared `worktree_model.py` vocabulary
    has no slot for "the query itself failed", so that distinction is
    carried as a separate fact here and resolved by the caller, never
    inside `disposition_for`).
    """
    merge_evidence = "not_checked"
    ls_remote_status = "not_checked"
    pr_state = "not_checked"
    pr_query_errored = False

    if entry.branch is not None:
        merge_evidence = "merged" if _is_ancestor(repo, entry.branch, base_ref) else "unmerged"
        if allow_network:
            ls_remote_status = _ls_remote_status(repo, entry.branch)
        if gh_ok:
            pr_state, pr_query_errored = _pr_state(repo, entry.branch)

    # DS-196: local-only, no network/gh dependency - computed unconditionally
    # (cheap: a single `git branch -r --contains`) regardless of
    # --no-origin-reachable-evidence, which only affects the evidence ORDER
    # passed to disposition_for, never whether this fact gets computed.
    origin_reachable = _compute_origin_reachable(repo, entry.head)

    facts = DispositionFacts(
        dirty_status=dirty_status,
        head_reachable="not_checked",
        ls_remote_status=ls_remote_status,
        merge_evidence=merge_evidence,
        content_subsumption="not_checked",
        pr_state=pr_state,
        origin_reachable=origin_reachable,
    )
    return facts, pr_query_errored


def _display_reason(facts: DispositionFacts) -> str:
    """Informational only - does not affect the decision, which is fully
    delegated to `disposition_for`. Mirrors that function's own evidence
    precedence for a human-readable --explain label.
    """
    if facts.merge_evidence == "merged":
        return "ancestor-of-base"
    if facts.pr_state == "MERGED":
        return "pr-merged"
    # DS-196: checked after merge_evidence and pr_state == "MERGED", mirroring
    # WORKTREE_REMOVAL_EVIDENCE_ORDER's own precedence (origin_reachable sits
    # after pr_state).
    if facts.origin_reachable == "reachable":
        return "origin-reachable"
    return "eligible"


def evaluate_entry(
    repo: str,
    entry,
    wt_class: WorktreeClass,
    base_ref: str,
    gh_ok: bool,
    allow_network: bool,
    min_age_hours: float,
    strict_ignored: bool,
    activity_window_hours: float = 3.0,
    no_origin_reachable_evidence: bool = False,
) -> Dict:
    """Resolves one worktree entry. `outcome` is one of: SKIP_UNMANAGED,
    SKIP_MISSING, SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED, SKIP_SELF, SKIP_TOO_YOUNG,
    SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY, SKIP_LOCKED, SKIP_DIRTY, SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT,
    SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR, SKIP_UNPROVEN, REMOVE.

    DS-196: `activity_window_hours` gates a worktree with a file touched
    inside the window (`SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY`) immediately after the
    `min_age_hours` age floor, before any git/gh evidence is gathered -
    same "protect a resumable-idle worktree" spirit as the age floor, but
    keyed on FILE content mtimes rather than the worktree root directory's
    own mtime. `no_origin_reachable_evidence` (the `--no-origin-reachable-
    evidence` CLI rollback lever), when True, makes the `disposition_for`
    call below pass `merge_evidence_order=MERGE_EVIDENCE_ORDER` explicitly
    - reproducing pre-DS-196 evidence order exactly.
    """
    base = {"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": wt_class.value}

    if wt_class is WorktreeClass.UNMANAGED:
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_UNMANAGED"}

    exists = Path(entry.path).is_dir()
    if not exists:
        # Admin-only entry (directory already gone). Unlock if locked (the
        # ONLY case the guardrail permits unlocking - its agent cannot
        # still be running when the directory is gone) so `git worktree
        # prune` at the end can actually reclaim it.
        if entry.locked:
            return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED"}
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_MISSING"}

    if _is_self_worktree(entry.path):
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_SELF"}

    age_hours = _worktree_age_hours(entry.path)
    if age_hours is None or age_hours < min_age_hours:
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_TOO_YOUNG"}

    # DS-196: gate 4.5, immediately after the age floor, before any git
    # status/evidence call - a None (indeterminate) reading fails CLOSED,
    # same posture as the age floor immediately above.
    activity_hours = _worktree_activity_hours(entry.path)
    if activity_hours is None or activity_hours < activity_window_hours:
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY"}

    dirty_status, ignored_offenders = _git_status_and_ignored(entry.path, strict_ignored)
    facts, pr_query_errored = _build_facts(repo, entry, dirty_status, base_ref, gh_ok, allow_network)
    disposition_kwargs = {}
    if no_origin_reachable_evidence:
        disposition_kwargs["merge_evidence_order"] = MERGE_EVIDENCE_ORDER
    disposition = disposition_for(entry, wt_class, facts, **disposition_kwargs)

    if disposition is Disposition.SKIP_LOCKED:
        # Guardrail: never unlock or force-remove a worktree whose
        # directory still exists - its agent may still be running (and
        # absence of a lock elsewhere does not imply this one is stale -
        # see the module docstring's Locked handling note).
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_LOCKED"}
    if disposition is Disposition.SKIP_DIRTY:
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_DIRTY"}
    if ignored_offenders:
        return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT", "offenders": ignored_offenders[:5]}
    # MAJOR fix: a transient `gh` PR-query FAILURE is a distinct fact from
    # "no PR exists" (see `_pr_state`'s docstring). `disposition_for` has no
    # way to see `pr_query_errored` - it only sees `facts.pr_state`, which
    # this tool sets to "not_checked" on a query failure, identical to what
    # it would be with `gh` fully unavailable. That is fine EXCEPT when the
    # resulting disposition is one this tool's own `--archive-unproven`
    # whitelist (`_ARCHIVABLE_UNPROVEN_DISPOSITIONS`) would later treat as
    # "the branch content itself is the ONLY unresolved question" -
    # `SKIP_NOT_PUSHED` or `SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR`. A query failure on
    # EITHER of those means the branch evidence is not actually fully
    # resolved (there may be a live OPEN PR the query simply could not see),
    # so this reclassifies to its OWN outcome bucket - never `SKIP_UNPROVEN`
    # - which is therefore excluded from the archive whitelist by
    # construction, not merely by an unlisted `reason` value. Mirrors the
    # `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR` precedent: a query failure is never silently
    # treated as proof of anything.
    if pr_query_errored and disposition in (Disposition.SKIP_NOT_PUSHED, Disposition.SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR):
        return {
            **base,
            "outcome": "SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR",
            "reason": f"gh pr query failed (would-be {disposition.value})",
        }
    if disposition is Disposition.ELIGIBLE:
        return {**base, "outcome": "REMOVE", "reason": _display_reason(facts)}
    return {**base, "outcome": "SKIP_UNPROVEN", "reason": disposition.value}


def _evaluate_entries(
    repo: str,
    entries,
    resolved_base: str,
    gh_ok: bool,
    allow_network: bool,
    min_age_hours: float,
    strict_ignored: bool,
    activity_window_hours: float = 3.0,
    no_origin_reachable_evidence: bool = False,
) -> List[Dict]:
    """The shared per-entry evaluation loop: entry 0 is always the MAIN
    worktree (`SKIP_MAIN`), every other entry is resolved via
    `evaluate_entry`. Factored out of the single-repo sweep body so the
    `--report` DEEP tier can compute the identical per-entry decision
    without duplicating this loop - both call sites pass the SAME
    `evaluate_entry`, so they cannot diverge on what counts as REMOVE.
    """
    results: List[Dict] = []
    for idx, entry in enumerate(entries):
        is_main = idx == 0
        wt_class = classify_entry(entry, host=repo, repo_root=repo, is_main=is_main)
        if is_main:
            results.append({"path": entry.path, "branch": entry.branch, "class": "MAIN", "outcome": "SKIP_MAIN"})
            continue
        results.append(
            evaluate_entry(
                repo,
                entry,
                wt_class,
                resolved_base,
                gh_ok,
                allow_network,
                min_age_hours,
                strict_ignored,
                activity_window_hours,
                no_origin_reachable_evidence,
            )
        )
    return results


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-repo discovery (DS-cleanup-worktrees-multi-repo Unit 2; the standalone
# bin/ds-reap-all wrapper this originally ported from was retired in Unit 3
# once this in-process implementation fully subsumed it).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH = Path("~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json").expanduser()
_MULTI_REPO_MAX_DEPTH = 3


def _init_config() -> int:
    """`--init-config`: scaffolds `~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json` for a
    first-time `--multi-repo` setup. NEVER overwrites an existing file -
    prints its current contents and exits 0 instead, so a re-run is always
    safe. On an absent file, creates `~/.agentic/` (parents, exist_ok) and
    writes an EMPTY `{"roots": [], "repos": []}` skeleton atomically (a
    `.tmp` sibling then `os.replace`, so a failure mid-write can never leave
    a truncated/partial config on disk). The printed instructions are
    explicit that this SessionStart machine-wide check will not actually
    activate until the operator adds at least one root or repo - an empty
    skeleton alone changes nothing.
    """
    if _MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH.is_file():
        try:
            current = _MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except OSError as exc:
            print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: could not read {_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH} already exists - not overwriting. Current contents:")
        print(current)
        return 0

    try:
        _MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp_path = _MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH.with_suffix(_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH.suffix + ".tmp")
        tmp_path.write_text(json.dumps({"roots": [], "repos": []}, indent=2) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
        os.replace(tmp_path, _MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH)
    except OSError as exc:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: could not write {_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: created {_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH} (empty).")
    print(
        'Add at least one "roots" or "repos" entry before this config does anything - the '
        "SessionStart machine-wide check will not activate on an empty skeleton. Example:"
    )
    print('  { "roots": ["~/Documents/Development"], "repos": [] }')
    return 0


class RepoTarget:
    """One repo the multi-repo sweep/report will attempt (or has already
    rejected at discovery time). `canonical` is the RESOLVED GIT TOPLEVEL
    path - `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`'s own stdout, re-resolved via
    `Path.resolve()` (follows symlinks, normalizes `..`/`.`) - never merely
    the operator-supplied path resolved on its own. This matters whenever
    an operator (or `~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json`) names a
    SUBDIRECTORY of a repo rather than its root: `Path(path).resolve()`
    alone would leave `canonical` pointing at that subdirectory, which
    `_run_repo` and `_run_report` then pass to `git -C <canonical> ...` -
    every one of those calls resolves relative to the subdirectory,
    `git worktree list --porcelain` still enumerates the whole repo's
    worktrees (git always does), but every other `git -C` call in this
    file and in `disposition_for`'s evidence-gathering is happy to treat a
    subdirectory identically to the toplevel where it matters for output
    (paths, dedup) - so a bare subdirectory silently produced a DIFFERENT
    verdict than the same input in single-repo mode (round-N Major 3:
    measured `--repo <repo>/sub --dry-run --no-gh` giving
    `removed=1 skipped-dirty=1` in single-repo mode but `removed=0
    skipped-unmanaged=2` under `--multi-repo` for the identical input).
    Capturing the toplevel here makes `canonical` agree with what
    single-repo mode's own `_resolve_repo` already returns, and makes
    dedup correctly collapse two different subdirectories of the same
    repo to one target.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: str, source: str):
        self.path = path
        self.source = source  # "explicit" | "scan" | "config"
        self.canonical: Optional[Path] = None
        self.discovery_error: Optional[str] = None

        try:
            expanded = Path(path).expanduser().resolve()
        except OSError as exc:
            self.discovery_error = f"could not resolve path: {exc}"
            return

        if not expanded.is_dir():
            self.discovery_error = f"not a directory: {path}"
            return

        proc = _run(["git", "-C", str(expanded), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            self.discovery_error = f"not a git repository: {path}"
            return

        toplevel = proc.stdout.strip()
        if not toplevel:
            self.discovery_error = f"git rev-parse --show-toplevel returned no output for: {path}"
            return

        try:
            self.canonical = Path(toplevel).resolve()
        except OSError as exc:
            self.discovery_error = f"could not resolve git toplevel path {toplevel!r}: {exc}"


def _is_git_dir(path: Path) -> bool:
    git_marker = path / ".git"
    return git_marker.is_dir() or git_marker.is_file()


def _scan_root(root: Path, depth: int) -> Tuple[List[Path], int]:
    """Returns `(found_repos, skipped_not_git)`. Scans `root`'s children to
    `depth` levels (depth 1 = immediate children only). A directory that IS
    a git repo is added and never descended into further. A directory that
    is NOT a git repo and has no more depth budget to descend is counted as
    `skipped_not_git`; one still eligible to descend further (depth
    remaining > 1) is explored instead of counted, since it may itself
    contain a repo at a deeper level.
    """
    found: List[Path] = []
    skipped_not_git = 0

    def walk(directory: Path, remaining_depth: int) -> None:
        nonlocal skipped_not_git
        try:
            children = sorted(p for p in directory.iterdir() if p.is_dir())
        except OSError:
            return
        for child in children:
            if _is_git_dir(child):
                found.append(child)
                continue
            if remaining_depth > 1:
                walk(child, remaining_depth - 1)
            else:
                skipped_not_git += 1

    walk(root, depth)
    return found, skipped_not_git


def _load_multi_repo_config() -> Dict:
    if not _MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH.is_file():
        return {}
    try:
        with open(_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            data = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        print(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: could not read config {_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH}: {exc}; treating as empty.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return {}
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        print(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: config {_MULTI_REPO_CONFIG_PATH} must be a JSON object - got {data!r}; "
            "treating as empty.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return {}
    return data


def _config_string_list(config: Dict, key: str) -> List[str]:
    """Returns `config[key]` as a `List[str]`, or `[]` when the key is
    absent. A present-but-malformed value (not a list at all, or a list
    containing a non-string element) is reported to stderr and treated as
    empty for that key - never an uncaught exception, never a silent
    per-character explosion of a bare string.
    """
    if key not in config:
        return []
    value = config[key]
    if not isinstance(value, list) or any(not isinstance(item, str) for item in value):
        print(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: ~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json field \"{key}\" must be a list of "
            f"strings - got {value!r}; ignoring this field.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return []
    return list(value)


def discover_repos_multi(
    explicit_repo_paths: List[str], root_paths: List[str], depth: int
) -> Tuple[List[RepoTarget], int, List[str]]:
    """Returns `(targets, skipped_not_git, root_errors)`.

    Explicit `--repo` entries and positional root directories are ADDITIVE
    explicit sources - both are honored when both are given in the same
    invocation, and the result is deduped by canonical path (see `dedupe`).
    The config-file fallback (`~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json`) is
    consulted ONLY when neither an explicit `--repo` nor a positional root
    was given at all.
    """
    skipped_not_git = 0
    root_errors: List[str] = []

    if not explicit_repo_paths and not root_paths:
        config = _load_multi_repo_config()
        explicit_repo_paths = _config_string_list(config, "repos")
        root_paths = _config_string_list(config, "roots")

    targets: List[RepoTarget] = [RepoTarget(p, "explicit") for p in explicit_repo_paths]

    for root_str in root_paths:
        root = Path(root_str).expanduser()
        if not root.is_dir():
            root_errors.append(f"root not a directory: {root_str}")
            continue
        found, skipped = _scan_root(root, depth)
        skipped_not_git += skipped
        for repo_path in found:
            targets.append(RepoTarget(str(repo_path), "scan"))

    return targets, skipped_not_git, root_errors


def dedupe(targets: List[RepoTarget]) -> List[RepoTarget]:
    """First-seen-wins dedup by canonical resolved path. A target whose
    path could not be resolved at all (`canonical is None`) is never
    deduped against anything else - it is kept as its own distinct
    (already-errored) entry so its discovery error is still reported.
    """
    seen: set = set()
    result: List[RepoTarget] = []
    for target in targets:
        if target.canonical is None:
            result.append(target)
            continue
        if target.canonical in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(target.canonical)
        result.append(target)
    return result


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --report (DS-cleanup-worktrees-multi-repo Unit 2). Structurally read-only:
# neither tier below ever calls _salvage_and_remove, "git worktree remove",
# "git worktree unlock", or "git worktree prune" - there is no removal code
# path reachable from here at all, not merely a --dry-run-shaped one.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _fast_report_row(repo: str) -> Tuple[Optional[Dict], Optional[str]]:
    """FAST/approximate tier (`--report --count-only`): exactly the 2 git
    calls named in the module docstring - `rev-parse --show-toplevel`
    (already paid by `RepoTarget.__init__` during discovery) plus
    `worktree list --porcelain` here - and zero network calls. Returns
    `(row, error)`; `row["eligible"]` is always None in this tier (never
    computed). This tier never runs `evaluate_entry`, so it has no
    disposition to measure size against - the `--measure-size` fields
    (DS-216) are always `None` here, same tier-dependent shape as
    `eligible`, never populated even when `--measure-size` is passed.
    """
    try:
        porcelain = _worktree_list(repo)
        entries = parse_porcelain(porcelain)
    except (RuntimeError, ValueError) as exc:
        return None, str(exc)

    nonroot = entries[1:]
    ages = [a for a in (_worktree_age_hours(e.path) for e in nonroot) if a is not None]
    oldest_age = max(ages) if ages else None
    return {
        "repo": repo,
        "nonroot_worktrees": len(nonroot),
        "oldest_age_hours": oldest_age,
        "eligible": None,
        "would_reclaim_kb": None,
        "dirty_unreclaimable_kb": None,
        "size_unknown_count": None,
    }, None


def _deep_report_row(repo: str, args: argparse.Namespace) -> Tuple[Optional[Dict], Optional[str]]:
    """DEEP/accurate tier (`--report`, no `--count-only`): a full per-entry
    `evaluate_entry` pass, same cost as `--dry-run --explain` today, but
    never acting on the result - `_evaluate_entries` is the SAME loop the
    single-repo sweep uses, so this tier cannot diverge on what counts as
    REMOVE. Returns `(row, error)`.
    """
    try:
        porcelain = _worktree_list(repo)
        entries = parse_porcelain(porcelain)
    except (RuntimeError, ValueError) as exc:
        return None, str(exc)

    if not entries:
        zero_size = 0 if args.measure_size else None
        return {
            "repo": repo,
            "nonroot_worktrees": 0,
            "oldest_age_hours": None,
            "eligible": 0,
            "would_reclaim_kb": zero_size,
            "dirty_unreclaimable_kb": zero_size,
            "size_unknown_count": zero_size,
        }, None

    # Round-N Minor fix: the deep tier used to hardcode `oldest_age_hours`
    # to None unconditionally, contradicting the documented `--json` shape
    # (`"oldest_age_hours": float`, with only `eligible` carved out as
    # tier-dependent). This tier already enumerates every non-root entry
    # (`entries[1:]`) below, so computing the same free `os.stat`-based age
    # the fast tier already computes costs nothing extra here - populate it
    # rather than leaving the two tiers disagree with the documented shape.
    nonroot_paths = [e.path for e in entries[1:]]
    ages = [a for a in (_worktree_age_hours(p) for p in nonroot_paths) if a is not None]
    oldest_age = max(ages) if ages else None

    resolved_base, _base_source, base_diagnostics = resolve_base_branch(repo, None, multi_repo=True)
    for line in base_diagnostics:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr)
    if resolved_base is None:
        return None, "base could not be resolved"

    gh_ok = _gh_available(repo, args.no_gh)
    allow_network = not args.no_gh
    results = _evaluate_entries(
        repo,
        entries,
        resolved_base,
        gh_ok,
        allow_network,
        args.min_age_hours,
        args.strict_ignored,
        args.activity_window_hours,
        args.no_origin_reachable_evidence,
    )
    eligible = sum(1 for r in results if r["outcome"] == "REMOVE")

    # --measure-size (DS-216): report mode is structurally read-only (see
    # this section's own module comment - never calls
    # `_salvage_and_remove`/`git worktree remove`), so there is no
    # "reclaimed" here, only a projection - `would_reclaim_kb`, never
    # `reclaimed_kb`, mirrors the same --dry-run-vs-live label discipline
    # `_run_repo`'s sweep enforces. `None` (not 0) when `--measure-size`
    # was not passed, matching the tier-dependent `eligible` shape above.
    would_reclaim_kb: Optional[int] = None
    dirty_unreclaimable_kb: Optional[int] = None
    size_unknown_count: Optional[int] = None
    if args.measure_size:
        would_reclaim_kb = 0
        dirty_unreclaimable_kb = 0
        size_unknown_count = 0
        for r in results:
            if r["outcome"] == "REMOVE":
                size_kb = _measure_size_kb(r["path"])
                if size_kb is None:
                    size_unknown_count += 1
                else:
                    would_reclaim_kb += size_kb
            elif r["outcome"] == "SKIP_DIRTY":
                size_kb = _measure_size_kb(r["path"])
                if size_kb is None:
                    size_unknown_count += 1
                else:
                    dirty_unreclaimable_kb += size_kb

    return {
        "repo": repo,
        "nonroot_worktrees": len(entries) - 1,
        "oldest_age_hours": oldest_age,
        "eligible": eligible,
        "would_reclaim_kb": would_reclaim_kb,
        "dirty_unreclaimable_kb": dirty_unreclaimable_kb,
        "size_unknown_count": size_unknown_count,
    }, None


def _repo_truncation_note(max_repos: int, total: int) -> str:
    """Shared `--max-repos` truncation NOTE text, printed identically by
    both `_run_report`'s human-readable table and `_run_multi_repo`'s plain
    sweep path - a single source instead of two independently-maintained
    literal copies (DS-189 finalization review debt)."""
    return (
        f"NOTE: repo discovery truncated to first {max_repos} of "
        f"{total} discovered repos (--max-repos {max_repos}) - "
        "unlisted repos were not evaluated."
    )


def _run_report(
    targets: List[RepoTarget],
    root_errors: List[str],
    args: argparse.Namespace,
    truncated: bool = False,
    discovered_count: Optional[int] = None,
) -> int:
    rows: List[Dict] = []
    errored = False

    for target in targets:
        if target.discovery_error is not None:
            print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {target.discovery_error}", file=sys.stderr)
            errored = True
            continue
        repo = str(target.canonical)
        if args.count_only:
            row, err = _fast_report_row(repo)
        else:
            row, err = _deep_report_row(repo, args)
        if err:
            print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {repo}: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            errored = True
            continue
        rows.append(row)

    if args.count_only:
        # Round-N operator decision: tiebreak by oldest_age_hours DESC, not
        # ASC - see the module docstring's `--report` `--count-only` tier
        # description for the full rationale (the original ASC tiebreak
        # ranked the repo with the YOUNGER oldest worktree as worse on a
        # count tie, which is backwards). A None age (unreachable in
        # practice - every enumerated worktree directory can be stat'd)
        # still sorts last under DESC via `-inf`.
        rows.sort(
            key=lambda r: (
                -r["nonroot_worktrees"],
                -(r["oldest_age_hours"] if r["oldest_age_hours"] is not None else float("-inf")),
            )
        )
    else:
        # Round-N Minor 3 fix: give the deep tier the SAME oldest_age_hours
        # DESC tiebreak the fast tier documents (see the fast-tier sort key
        # above for the full rationale) - previously an `eligible` tie fell
        # back to undocumented discovery order (alphabetical, via
        # `_scan_root`'s own `sorted()`), so two repos comparing the two
        # tables side by side could see rows reorder for no visible reason.
        # A None age (unreachable in practice) still sorts last under DESC
        # via `-inf`, identically to the fast tier.
        rows.sort(
            key=lambda r: (
                -(r["eligible"] or 0),
                -(r["oldest_age_hours"] if r["oldest_age_hours"] is not None else float("-inf")),
            )
        )

    if args.json:
        # Round-N Major 5 fix: the printed TABLE already labels its tier
        # explicitly (the FAST/approximate vs DEEP/accurate banner lines
        # below), but the JSON used to emit a bare array with no equivalent
        # signal - a machine consumer had no way to tell a fast-tier
        # `eligible: null` approximation from a deep-tier result without
        # separately tracking which flags produced it. Wrap the rows in an
        # object carrying an explicit `tier` field so JSON and table agree.
        tier = "fast" if args.count_only else "deep"
        print(json.dumps({"tier": tier, "rows": rows, "truncated": truncated}))
    else:
        if args.count_only:
            print(
                "ds-cleanup-worktrees --report --count-only: FAST/approximate tier - raw "
                "worktree count + directory age only, NOT a measure of staleness. Run "
                "--report (without --count-only) for an accurate eligible-for-removal count."
            )
            header = f"{'repo':<60} {'nonroot':>7} {'oldest_age_hrs':>15}"
        else:
            print(
                "ds-cleanup-worktrees --report: DEEP/accurate tier - full per-entry "
                "evaluation, eligible-for-removal count."
            )
            header = f"{'repo':<60} {'nonroot':>7} {'eligible':>9}"
        print(header)
        for r in rows:
            if args.count_only:
                age = f"{r['oldest_age_hours']:.1f}" if r["oldest_age_hours"] is not None else "n/a"
                print(f"{r['repo']:<60} {r['nonroot_worktrees']:>7} {age:>15}")
            else:
                print(f"{r['repo']:<60} {r['nonroot_worktrees']:>7} {r['eligible']:>9}")
        # Round-2 Minor fix: truncation was previously visible only via the
        # `--report --json` `"truncated"` key - the human-readable table
        # printed nothing, so an operator reading raw `--report` output had
        # no signal that repos beyond --max-repos were never evaluated.
        if truncated:
            total = discovered_count if discovered_count is not None else len(rows)
            print(_repo_truncation_note(args.max_repos, total))

    return 1 if (errored or root_errors) else 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_args(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> argparse.Namespace:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="ds-cleanup-worktrees",
        description="Executable implementation of the /ds-cleanup-worktrees removal predicate.",
    )
    p.add_argument("--repo", action="append", default=[])
    p.add_argument("roots", nargs="*", default=[])
    p.add_argument("--multi-repo", dest="multi_repo", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--depth", type=int, default=None, choices=[1, 2, 3])
    p.add_argument("--report", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--base", default=None)
    p.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--no-gh", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--explain", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--count-only", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--min-age-hours", type=float, default=24.0)
    p.add_argument("--activity-window-hours", type=float, default=3.0)
    p.add_argument("--no-origin-reachable-evidence", dest="no_origin_reachable_evidence", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--strict-ignored", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--archive-unproven", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--measure-size", dest="measure_size", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--init-config", dest="init_config", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--max-repos", type=int, default=None)
    return p.parse_args(argv)


def _validate_args(args: argparse.Namespace) -> Optional[str]:
    """Returns a usage-error message, or None when valid. Called BEFORE any
    git subprocess call - see the module docstring's `--multi-repo`/
    `--report` sections for the full rationale behind each check.
    """
    if not args.multi_repo:
        if len(args.repo) > 1:
            return "multiple --repo requires --multi-repo"
        if args.roots:
            return "positional root arguments require --multi-repo"
        if args.depth is not None:
            return "--depth requires --multi-repo"
        if args.report:
            return "--report requires --multi-repo (a single-repo report is just --dry-run --explain)"
    if args.multi_repo and args.base is not None:
        return "--base cannot be combined with --multi-repo - each repo resolves its own base independently"
    if args.report and args.archive_unproven:
        return "--report is incompatible with --archive-unproven (report mode is structurally read-only)"
    if args.json and not args.report:
        return "--json requires --report"
    if args.max_repos is not None and not args.multi_repo:
        return "--max-repos requires --multi-repo"
    # Round-2 Major fix: a 0 or negative --max-repos silently mis-slices
    # `targets[: args.max_repos]` (negative drops the LAST N entries and
    # still reports `truncated: true`; 0 empties the list, producing the
    # misleading "no repos discovered" usage error instead of a clear
    # complaint about the flag itself). Reject both before any discovery
    # or evaluation work happens.
    if args.max_repos is not None and args.max_repos < 1:
        return "--max-repos must be >= 1"
    return None


#: Every outcome `evaluate_entry`/the MAIN-entry/UNMANAGED-entry branches in
#: `main()` can produce - used to assert the bucket-sum-equals-entries
#: invariant (round-2 Minor a) and to build the summary line's field set.
_ALL_OUTCOMES = (
    "SKIP_MAIN",
    "SKIP_UNMANAGED",
    "SKIP_MISSING",
    "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED",
    "SKIP_SELF",
    "SKIP_TOO_YOUNG",
    "SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY",
    "SKIP_LOCKED",
    "SKIP_DIRTY",
    "SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT",
    "SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR",
    "SKIP_UNPROVEN",
    "SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED",
    "ARCHIVED_AND_REMOVED",
    "REMOVE",
)


def _field_name(outcome: str) -> str:
    """`SKIP_TOO_YOUNG` -> `skipped-too-young` for the summary line."""
    return outcome.lower().replace("skip_", "skipped-").replace("_", "-")


def _run_repo(
    repo: str, args: argparse.Namespace, _base_unresolved_out: Optional[List[bool]] = None
) -> int:
    """The single-repo sweep body - unchanged in behavior from before
    `--multi-repo`/`--report` existed, INCLUDING its exit-code contract
    (`bin/ds-base-sync` and `hooks/session-start-wrap.sh` depend on it -
    see the module docstring's Failure modes section). `repo` is already a
    resolved git toplevel path (resolved by the caller: `main()` for
    single-repo mode via `_resolve_repo`, or `_run_multi_repo` per-target
    via `RepoTarget.__init__`'s own `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` call).
    Called once per repo, sequentially, by both modes - this is the ONLY
    place any of this file's removal/archive/prune logic lives.

    `_base_unresolved_out` (round-N Major 1 fix): an OPTIONAL caller-owned
    output list, unused by single-repo callers (default None, and this
    function's own return value is completely unaffected either way - it
    still returns 0 on an unresolvable base, exactly as before). When
    `_run_multi_repo` passes a list, this function appends `True` to it
    IFF this run hit the "base could not be resolved" branch below -
    distinguishing "swept cleanly" from "skipped: base unresolvable" at
    the AGGREGATION layer without changing this function's own signature-
    level exit-code meaning. Before this fix, `_run_multi_repo` had no way
    to see this distinction at all: it only saw `rc == 0`, identical to a
    fully swept repo, so a base-unresolvable repo silently counted as
    `swept=1 errored=0` with exit 0 - contradicting both the documented
    multi-repo exit contract ("1 when at least one repo or root had a
    discovery or evaluation error") and `--report`, which already exits 1
    on the identical condition (`_deep_report_row` treats it as an `err`).
    """
    try:
        porcelain = _worktree_list(repo)
        entries = parse_porcelain(porcelain)
    except (RuntimeError, ValueError) as exc:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - internal error, report and exit 1
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: internal error: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # --count-only (round-2 Major 4): zero network calls, zero per-entry
    # git calls - the SINGLE `git worktree list --porcelain` call above is
    # the entire cost. Both automatic call sites (ds-base-sync, the
    # SessionStart nudge) use this mode exclusively.
    if args.count_only:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: mode=count-only entries={len(entries)}")
        return 0

    # Round-4 rework: base-branch resolution runs here, strictly AFTER the
    # --count-only early return above, so that mode's zero-added-git-calls
    # contract (bin/ds-base-sync, hooks/session-start-wrap.sh both depend
    # on it) is unaffected by this tool now resolving --base itself.
    resolved_base, base_source, base_diagnostics = resolve_base_branch(
        repo, args.base, multi_repo=args.multi_repo
    )
    for line in base_diagnostics:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr)
    if resolved_base is None:
        # Fails SAFE, not fatal: this mirrors the asymmetry the shell
        # pipeline this replaces already had (a resolved-but-invalid base
        # printed a WARNING and skipped the reap for the session WITHOUT
        # exiting nonzero, so a caller's subsequent steps still ran) - exit
        # 1 stays reserved for a genuine internal/usage error (bad --repo,
        # an unhandled exception), never "no base could be resolved this
        # run." Every candidate tried is already named in base_diagnostics
        # above (printed to stderr).
        print("ds-cleanup-worktrees: base could not be resolved - reap skipped this run.", file=sys.stderr)
        if _base_unresolved_out is not None:
            _base_unresolved_out.append(True)
        return 0
    if base_source != "explicit":
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: base auto-resolved to '{resolved_base}' via {base_source}")

    # Round-6 (Skeptic Major 2): `--archive-unproven` REFUSES to run at all
    # in degraded gh mode - a real `gh_ok`/`degraded` computed here, ahead
    # of the (rare) empty-entries branch below, so the refusal check and
    # every other consumer of `degraded` share the SAME evidence, never a
    # second independent `_gh_available` call that could disagree with it.
    # `--count-only` above already returned before this point, so this
    # computation still costs zero network calls in that mode.
    gh_ok = _gh_available(repo, args.no_gh)
    allow_network = not args.no_gh
    degraded = not gh_ok

    if args.archive_unproven and degraded:
        print(
            "ds-cleanup-worktrees: --archive-unproven requires PR evidence (gh) and refuses to "
            "run in degraded mode (gh unavailable/unauthenticated/erroring/--no-gh) - "
            "archiving without PR evidence cannot reliably distinguish a genuinely-unprovable "
            "branch from one behind an open PR. Re-run with `gh` authenticated and without "
            "--no-gh, or omit --archive-unproven.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1

    if not entries:
        # Practically unreachable against a real git repo (`git worktree
        # list --porcelain` always emits at least the main worktree), but
        # the mode string is still composed from the SAME dry-run/degraded
        # axes as the populated-entries path below, not hardcoded "live".
        empty_mode_parts = []
        if degraded:
            empty_mode_parts.append("degraded (gh unavailable)")
        if args.dry_run:
            empty_mode_parts.append("dry-run")
        empty_mode = ", ".join(empty_mode_parts) if empty_mode_parts else "live"
        empty_fields = " ".join(f"{_field_name(o)}=0" for o in _ALL_OUTCOMES if o != "REMOVE")
        print(
            f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: base={resolved_base} mode={empty_mode} entries=0 removed=0 "
            f"{empty_fields} pruned-admin=0 salvaged=0"
        )
        return 0

    results = _evaluate_entries(
        repo,
        entries,
        resolved_base,
        gh_ok,
        allow_network,
        args.min_age_hours,
        args.strict_ignored,
        args.activity_window_hours,
        args.no_origin_reachable_evidence,
    )

    # Bucket-membership invariant (round-2 Minor a, round-6 correction):
    # every result must land in exactly one of _ALL_OUTCOMES - fail loudly
    # (internal error) rather than silently under/over-counting the summary
    # line. The old `sum(outcome_counts.values()) == len(results)` assert
    # below this comment was TAUTOLOGICAL - `Counter(r["outcome"] for r in
    # results)` sums to `len(results)` by construction regardless of what
    # the outcome strings actually are, so it could never fail; the
    # `unknown_outcomes` check above it is the only real guard here and is
    # kept alone.
    outcome_counts = Counter(r["outcome"] for r in results)
    unknown_outcomes = set(outcome_counts) - set(_ALL_OUTCOMES)
    if unknown_outcomes:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: internal error: unrecognized outcome(s) {sorted(unknown_outcomes)}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    remove_results = [r for r in results if r["outcome"] == "REMOVE"]

    # --measure-size (opt-in, DS-216): dirty entries are refused
    # REMOVAL terminally by design (see the module docstring's
    # `SKIP_DIRTY` gate) and are where the measured byte mass on a real
    # checkout actually lives - one repo carries ~32 GB of them. Measure
    # them here, before any removal work below, so `dirty_unreclaimable_kb`
    # is visible even though nothing about these entries changes this run.
    # This total must NEVER be folded into `reclaimed_kb`/`would_reclaim_kb`
    # below - reporting refused bytes as reclaimed would be exactly the
    # kind of lie this feature exists to prevent.
    size_unknown_count = 0
    dirty_unreclaimable_kb = 0
    if args.measure_size:
        for r in results:
            if r["outcome"] == "SKIP_DIRTY":
                size_kb = _measure_size_kb(r["path"])
                if size_kb is None:
                    r["size_unknown"] = True
                    size_unknown_count += 1
                else:
                    r["size_kb"] = size_kb
                    dirty_unreclaimable_kb += size_kb

    removed = 0
    salvaged = 0
    salvage_would = 0
    if not args.dry_run:
        for r in remove_results:
            path = r["path"]
            # Measure BEFORE removal - there is nothing left to measure
            # afterward. A `None` result (timeout/failure) is recorded as
            # size_unknown and excluded from `reclaimed_kb`, never
            # defaulted to 0 (see `_measure_size_kb`'s own docstring).
            if args.measure_size:
                size_kb = _measure_size_kb(path)
                if size_kb is None:
                    r["size_unknown"] = True
                    size_unknown_count += 1
                else:
                    r["size_kb"] = size_kb
            # Round-4 requirement, round-6 refactor (Skeptic Major 1): the
            # salvage-then-remove sequence now lives in ONE function,
            # `_salvage_and_remove`, shared with the --archive-unproven
            # loop below - see that function's docstring for why (round 5
            # shipped the archive loop bypassing salvage entirely by
            # calling `git worktree remove` directly).
            outcome = _salvage_and_remove(repo, path, r.get("branch"))
            if outcome["salvage_failed"]:
                print(
                    f"WARNING: telemetry salvage failed for {path} (branch={r.get('branch')}): "
                    f"{outcome['detail']} - worktree NOT removed",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                r["outcome"] = "SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT"
                r["offenders"] = [f"salvage-failed: {outcome['detail']}"]
                continue
            if outcome["salvaged"]:
                salvaged += 1
            if outcome["removed"]:
                removed += 1
                print(f"REMOVED {path} (branch={r['branch']}, reason={r['reason']})")
            else:
                # Round-6 Minor b: a failed `git worktree remove` used to
                # leave this entry's outcome as "REMOVE" - a bucket that is
                # deliberately EXCLUDED from the printed field_str (see
                # `_ALL_OUTCOMES`/field_str below), so a real removal
                # failure was invisible in the summary line and only
                # visible via the stderr WARNING. Reclassify to the
                # dedicated, VISIBLE `SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED` bucket instead.
                print(
                    f"WARNING: failed to remove {path} (git worktree remove failed): "
                    f"{outcome['detail']}",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                r["outcome"] = "SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED"
        # A salvage or remove failure above may have reclassified a REMOVE
        # entry to SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT or SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED after
        # `outcome_counts` was first computed - recompute so the summary
        # line reflects the FINAL state, never a stale pre-loop snapshot.
        # Every value this loop can assign is already a member of
        # _ALL_OUTCOMES, so no further unknown-outcome check is needed.
        outcome_counts = Counter(r["outcome"] for r in results)
    else:
        removed = len(remove_results)
        # --dry-run: skip salvage cleanly, but still report how many files
        # WOULD be salvaged if this were a live run (round-4 requirement).
        salvage_would = sum(1 for r in remove_results if _worktree_has_salvageable_telemetry(r["path"]))
        # --measure-size under --dry-run: measure the same REMOVE-bucket
        # entries a live run would, so `would_reclaim_kb` reflects an
        # actual `du` measurement, not an estimate.
        if args.measure_size:
            for r in remove_results:
                size_kb = _measure_size_kb(r["path"])
                if size_kb is None:
                    r["size_unknown"] = True
                    size_unknown_count += 1
                else:
                    r["size_kb"] = size_kb

    # --archive-unproven (round-5, OPT-IN, never the default; round-6
    # correction - Skeptic Major 2): for every entry that is still
    # SKIP_UNPROVEN AND whose underlying disposition (preserved in `reason`
    # by evaluate_entry) is on the EXPLICIT `_ARCHIVABLE_UNPROVEN_DISPOSITIONS`
    # whitelist - never the whole SKIP_UNPROVEN bucket, which also holds
    # `SKIP_PR_OPEN` (a hard safety override) and `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR` (a
    # transient failure), neither of which this flag may ever archive - by
    # evaluate_entry's own gate order, a whitelisted entry already passed
    # self/age/dirty/locked/protected-content and only failed the
    # branch-evidence gate: archive the FULL branch (compacted against
    # resolved_base when doing so still yields a non-empty bundle, DS-191)
    # into a verified git bundle, THEN salvage telemetry, and only THEN
    # remove the worktree
    # (via the SAME `_salvage_and_remove` the plain REMOVE loop above uses -
    # round 5 called `git worktree remove` directly here, bypassing salvage
    # entirely). A bundle-create/verify failure, or a telemetry-salvage
    # failure, never removes anything; the entry stays SKIP_UNPROVEN,
    # reported (a salvage failure after a successful archive leaves the
    # already-verified bundle in place - it is not undone).
    archived_and_removed = 0
    archive_would = 0
    if args.archive_unproven:
        unproven_results = [
            r
            for r in results
            if r["outcome"] == "SKIP_UNPROVEN" and r.get("reason") in _ARCHIVABLE_UNPROVEN_DISPOSITIONS
        ]
        if not args.dry_run:
            for r in unproven_results:
                path = r["path"]
                branch = r.get("branch")
                if not branch:
                    # No branch to bundle (detached HEAD) - nothing this
                    # leg can do; stays SKIP_UNPROVEN, already reported.
                    continue
                # Round-N (DS-191 MAJOR-2 fix): an explicit --base is
                # unvalidated by resolve_base_branch - route it through
                # _archive_compaction_base first so an arbitrary
                # caller-supplied ref is only compacted against when it
                # happens to coincide with the durable auto-resolved base
                # (see that function's docstring). `compaction_base is
                # None` here is the caller-side signal that compaction was
                # refused BEFORE even attempting exclusion - distinct from
                # `compact=False` below, which _archive_branch_bundle can
                # also return for its own internal reasons.
                compaction_base = _archive_compaction_base(repo, resolved_base, base_source)
                explicit_base_refused = base_source == "explicit" and compaction_base is None
                # MINOR-2 fix (round-3 review): `explicit_base_refused`
                # collapses two distinct causes that need different
                # remediation advice. Re-derive which one applies here
                # (read-only, does not affect `compaction_base` above) so
                # the NOTE below never tells an operator to omit --base
                # when doing so would make this tool print "base could
                # not be resolved - reap skipped this run" and do nothing
                # at all, rather than restoring compaction.
                auto_base_unresolvable = False
                if explicit_base_refused:
                    _auto_check, _auto_source_check, _auto_diag_check = resolve_base_branch(repo, None)
                    auto_base_unresolvable = _auto_check is None
                archive_ok, archive_detail, bundle_path, compact = _archive_branch_bundle(
                    repo, branch, compaction_base
                )
                if not archive_ok:
                    print(
                        f"WARNING: archive failed for {path} (branch={branch}): "
                        f"{archive_detail} - worktree NOT removed",
                        file=sys.stderr,
                    )
                    continue
                if not compact:
                    # Round-N (DS-191, corrected): every reachable
                    # not-compact cause at this call site is now covered
                    # below, not just two. `explicit_base_refused` is known
                    # for certain by the caller (computed above, before
                    # _archive_branch_bundle even runs) and gets its own,
                    # more specific message; every other cause funnels
                    # through _archive_branch_bundle's own return, which
                    # collapses several distinct internal reasons (base
                    # unverifiable, branch shares no history with it, the
                    # TOCTOU concurrent-fetch retry path, or a rev-list
                    # count that failed to parse) into the single `compact`
                    # boolean - see content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md.
                    if explicit_base_refused and auto_base_unresolvable:
                        print(
                            f"NOTE: archived {path} (branch={branch}) as a full-history bundle "
                            f"(base '{resolved_base}' was supplied explicitly via --base, but this "
                            "tool's own auto-resolution could not resolve ANY durable base for this "
                            "repo either, so there is nothing to compact against - full-history is "
                            "the only safe option right now; omitting --base would NOT restore "
                            "compaction here, it would make this tool skip the run entirely with "
                            "'base could not be resolved' - a durable base ref (e.g. origin/HEAD, "
                            "main, or a declared default branch) must exist first) - see "
                            f"content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md; bundle={bundle_path}",
                            file=sys.stderr,
                        )
                    elif explicit_base_refused:
                        print(
                            f"NOTE: archived {path} (branch={branch}) as a full-history bundle "
                            f"(base '{resolved_base}' was supplied explicitly via --base and does "
                            "not resolve to the same commit this tool's own auto-resolution would "
                            "have picked, so it is not guaranteed durable - compaction was skipped; "
                            "omit --base to let auto-resolution pick a durable base and restore "
                            "compaction) - see content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md; "
                            f"bundle={bundle_path}",
                            file=sys.stderr,
                        )
                    else:
                        print(
                            f"NOTE: archived {path} (branch={branch}) as a full-history bundle "
                            f"(base '{resolved_base}' could not be excluded - it may be "
                            f"unverifiable, share no history with '{branch}', have been advanced "
                            "past the branch tip by a concurrent fetch during archival, or its "
                            "rev-list count could not be measured) - see "
                            f"content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md; bundle={bundle_path}",
                            file=sys.stderr,
                        )
                outcome = _salvage_and_remove(repo, path, branch)
                if outcome["salvage_failed"]:
                    print(
                        f"WARNING: telemetry salvage failed for {path} (branch={branch}) after "
                        f"successful archive (bundle={bundle_path}): {outcome['detail']} - "
                        "worktree NOT removed",
                        file=sys.stderr,
                    )
                    continue
                if outcome["salvaged"]:
                    salvaged += 1
                if outcome["removed"]:
                    archived_and_removed += 1
                    r["outcome"] = "ARCHIVED_AND_REMOVED"
                    r["reason"] = f"archived to {bundle_path}"
                    restore_parenthetical = (
                        " (requires the commit git bundle actually recorded as this bundle's "
                        f"prerequisite - the boundary/fork-point commit between '{branch}' and "
                        f"'{resolved_base}' at archive time, NOT necessarily that ref's own tip - "
                        "to still be present in this repo's object store; run `git bundle verify "
                        "<bundle>` to see exactly which commit(s))"
                        if compact
                        else ""
                    )
                    print(
                        f"ARCHIVED+REMOVED {path} (branch={branch}) - restore with: "
                        f"{_restore_command(bundle_path, branch)}{restore_parenthetical}"
                    )
                else:
                    print(
                        f"WARNING: failed to remove {path} after successful archive "
                        f"(branch={branch}, bundle={bundle_path}): {outcome['detail']}",
                        file=sys.stderr,
                    )
                    r["outcome"] = "SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED"
            # Recompute for the same reason the salvage loop above does -
            # an archive success may have reclassified a SKIP_UNPROVEN
            # entry to ARCHIVED_AND_REMOVED or SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED, both
            # already members of _ALL_OUTCOMES.
            outcome_counts = Counter(r["outcome"] for r in results)
        else:
            # --dry-run: create nothing, report the would-be count only.
            archive_would = sum(1 for r in unproven_results if r.get("branch"))
            # Minor fix: `salvage_would` above was computed ONLY over
            # `remove_results` (the plain REMOVE bucket), before this
            # archive-unproven block even runs - so telemetry sitting in an
            # archive-eligible entry was never counted, contradicting the
            # "both paths run the same salvage-then-remove sequence"
            # docstring parity claim. Add the archive candidates' own
            # salvageable count so a `--dry-run --archive-unproven` NOTE is
            # not systematically undercounted.
            salvage_would += sum(1 for r in unproven_results if _worktree_has_salvageable_telemetry(r["path"]))

    # Unlock any directory-missing locked entries so the admin-prune pass
    # below can reclaim them (the sole permitted unlock case - see the
    # Locked handling note in the module docstring). Real action, real
    # unlock, so this stays dry-run-gated like the removal loop above.
    missing_locked = [r for r in results if r["outcome"] == "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED"]
    if not args.dry_run:
        for r in missing_locked:
            _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "unlock", r["path"]])

    # `pruned-admin` reports an ACTION TAKEN, not a candidate count (round-2
    # Minor c - v1 reported a nonzero value under --dry-run even though
    # `git worktree prune` was never invoked). It is 0 under --dry-run,
    # unconditionally; the candidate set remains fully visible via
    # SKIP_MISSING/SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED in the bucket counts and --explain.
    pruned_admin = 0
    if not args.dry_run:
        pruned_admin = sum(1 for r in results if r["outcome"] in ("SKIP_MISSING", "SKIP_MISSING_LOCKED"))
        _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "prune"])

    mode_parts = []
    if degraded:
        mode_parts.append("degraded (gh unavailable)")
    if args.dry_run:
        mode_parts.append("dry-run")
    mode = ", ".join(mode_parts) if mode_parts else "live"

    field_str = " ".join(
        f"{_field_name(outcome)}={outcome_counts.get(outcome, 0)}" for outcome in _ALL_OUTCOMES if outcome != "REMOVE"
    )

    # --measure-size (DS-216): the label MUST differ between --dry-run and
    # a live run - they mean different things (a projection vs. bytes
    # actually freed) and conflating them is how "we recovered X" becomes
    # a lie. Summed only over entries whose FINAL outcome is "REMOVE" -
    # under --dry-run that is every entry in `remove_results` (the removal
    # loop never runs, so nothing is reassigned); under a live run, a
    # salvage or removal failure above already reclassified the entry away
    # from "REMOVE" (SKIP_PROTECTED_CONTENT/SKIP_REMOVE_FAILED), so this
    # sum reflects only what was ACTUALLY removed, never an attempt.
    size_fields = ""
    if args.measure_size:
        reclaim_label = "would_reclaim_kb" if args.dry_run else "reclaimed_kb"
        reclaim_total_kb = sum(
            r["size_kb"] for r in results if r["outcome"] == "REMOVE" and r.get("size_kb") is not None
        )
        size_fields = (
            f" {reclaim_label}={reclaim_total_kb} dirty_unreclaimable_kb={dirty_unreclaimable_kb} "
            f"size_unknown_count={size_unknown_count}"
        )

    # `salvaged` (round-4): actual telemetry-salvage count, 0 under
    # --dry-run unconditionally - matches the `pruned-admin` "action
    # taken, not a candidate count" convention above.
    print(
        f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: base={resolved_base} mode={mode} entries={len(results)} "
        f"removed={removed} {field_str} pruned-admin={pruned_admin} salvaged={salvaged}{size_fields}"
    )

    if args.measure_size and size_unknown_count:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {size_unknown_count} worktree(s) could not be measured (`du -sk` failed or "
            f"timed out after {_MEASURE_SIZE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:g}s) - excluded from every total "
            "above, never counted as 0. Every byte total this run reports is therefore a FLOOR, "
            "not an exact figure, while size_unknown_count is nonzero."
        )

    if args.measure_size:
        print(
            "NOTE: byte totals above measure the working-tree checkout only, never the shared "
            "git object store (every worktree of a repo shares it) - removing a worktree does not "
            "shrink it, and deleting a branch reclaims nothing until `git gc` runs."
        )

    if degraded:
        print(
            "NOTE: degraded mode (gh unavailable/unauthenticated/erroring/--no-gh) - the "
            "PR-state leg is unavailable" + (" and git ls-remote was suppressed (--no-gh)" if args.no_gh else "")
            + "; this can only remove FEWER worktrees than a full run, never more."
        )

    # Minor fix: visibility parity with the degraded-mode NOTE above -
    # gh_ok being True (process-level auth fine) does not mean every
    # PER-ENTRY `gh pr list` call succeeded; without this, a nonzero
    # `skipped-pr-query-error` count was visible only in the bucket field
    # and `--explain`, while `mode` still read plain "live" with no
    # explanation printed.
    pr_query_error_count = outcome_counts.get("SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR", 0)
    if pr_query_error_count:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {pr_query_error_count} worktree(s) had a `gh pr list` query failure "
            "(rate limit/auth hiccup/network blip on that one call, even though gh itself is "
            "available) - never treated as proof of no PR; preserved and reported as "
            "SKIP_PR_QUERY_ERROR, never archived or removed."
        )

    # DS-189 Unit A: `SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR` is a Disposition VALUE, not an
    # outcome bucket - `evaluate_entry` folds it into
    # {"outcome": "SKIP_UNPROVEN", "reason": disposition.value}, so it
    # cannot be read off `outcome_counts` (which only has outcome-bucket
    # keys). Scan `results` for SKIP_UNPROVEN entries whose reason is
    # SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR instead. A high fraction of these means the
    # `git ls-remote` leg couldn't reach origin for most entries (network/
    # auth), which can look identical to a clean, fully-resolved sweep -
    # surface it whenever at least half of all entries hit it.
    ls_remote_error_count = sum(
        1 for r in results if r["outcome"] == "SKIP_UNPROVEN" and r.get("reason") == "SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR"
    )
    if ls_remote_error_count and len(results) and ls_remote_error_count / len(results) >= 0.5:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {ls_remote_error_count} of {len(results)} worktree(s) are SKIP_UNPROVEN "
            "(SKIP_LS_REMOTE_ERROR) - a `git ls-remote` failure reaching origin (network/auth, "
            "distinct from `gh` being unavailable), not proof the branch is unmerged. This can "
            "look like a clean tree while the branch-existence check could not actually run for "
            "most entries. Check network/auth to origin before trusting a low `removed` count "
            "from this run."
        )

    # Round-2 rework (Skeptic Major 1): an operator running this right after
    # a merge, at the default 24h age floor, sees `removed=0` in the summary
    # line above with no obvious explanation - the age floor is buried in
    # the `skipped-too-young` bucket field unless they already know to look
    # for it. Surface it as its own NOTE, same visibility tier as the
    # PR-query-error NOTE above, whenever the bucket is nonzero.
    too_young_count = outcome_counts.get("SKIP_TOO_YOUNG", 0)
    if too_young_count:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {too_young_count} worktree(s) skipped because they are younger than "
            f"the {args.min_age_hours:g}h age floor (--min-age-hours) - an unlocked worktree "
            "can still belong to a resumable session, so this floor is a safety default, not "
            "a bug. The age floor is only one of several gates, so a worktree may still be "
            "skipped afterward for another reason (dirty, locked, unproven branch, etc.) - "
            "narrowing this floor does not guarantee removal, and removes the protection "
            "against reaping a concurrent session's fresh worktree."
        )

    # DS-196: mirrors the SKIP_TOO_YOUNG NOTE above - a worktree with a file
    # touched inside --activity-window-hours is buried in the
    # `skipped-recent-activity` bucket field unless surfaced explicitly.
    recent_activity_count = outcome_counts.get("SKIP_RECENT_ACTIVITY", 0)
    if recent_activity_count:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {recent_activity_count} worktree(s) skipped because a file inside them was "
            f"touched within the {args.activity_window_hours:g}h activity window "
            "(--activity-window-hours) - a resumable, actively-used worktree, so this is a "
            "safety default, not a bug. The activity floor is only one of several gates, so a "
            "worktree may still be skipped afterward for another reason (dirty, locked, "
            "unproven branch, etc.) - narrowing this floor does not guarantee removal."
        )

    if args.dry_run and salvage_would:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {salvage_would} .agentic/events.jsonl file(s) would be salvaged to "
            ".agentic/reaped-telemetry/ before removal if this were a live run."
        )

    if args.archive_unproven and args.dry_run and archive_would:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {archive_would} unproven branch(es) would be archived to "
            ".agentic/worktree-archive/ (as a verified git bundle) and their worktrees "
            "removed if this were a live run with --archive-unproven."
        )

    if args.explain:
        print("-- per-entry --")
        for r in results:
            reason = f" ({r['reason']})" if "reason" in r else ""
            offenders = f" offenders={r['offenders']}" if "offenders" in r else ""
            size = ""
            if args.measure_size:
                if r.get("size_unknown"):
                    size = " size_unknown=true"
                elif "size_kb" in r:
                    size = f" size_kb={r['size_kb']}"
            print(f"{r['path']} [{r['class']}] branch={r['branch']}: {r['outcome']}{reason}{offenders}{size}")

    return 0


def _run_multi_repo(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    """`--multi-repo` entry point: discovers targets, then either runs the
    read-only `--report` path or sweeps every discovered repo sequentially
    via `_run_repo` - the SAME function single-repo mode uses, so the two
    modes cannot diverge on any removal/safety logic. See the module
    docstring's `--multi-repo` section for the full exit-code contract.
    """
    depth = args.depth if args.depth is not None else 1
    targets, skipped_not_git, root_errors = discover_repos_multi(list(args.repo), list(args.roots), depth)
    targets = dedupe(targets)

    # DS-189 Unit A: `--max-repos` bounds git-call COST (per-repo
    # discovery/evaluation), not display - applied AFTER dedup so it is
    # counted against the true discovered set, and BEFORE any per-repo
    # evaluation so an over-limit sweep or report never pays for the repos
    # it drops. Preserves discovery order (no re-sort). Absent = unbounded,
    # byte-identical to pre-DS-189 behavior.
    truncated = False
    discovered_count = len(targets)
    if args.max_repos is not None and len(targets) > args.max_repos:
        truncated = True
        targets = targets[: args.max_repos]

    for err in root_errors:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {err}", file=sys.stderr)

    if not targets and not root_errors:
        print(
            "ds-cleanup-worktrees: no repos discovered. Usage: ds-cleanup-worktrees --multi-repo "
            "[--repo <path> ...] [root ...] - or configure ~/.agentic/cleanup-worktrees.json with a "
            '"roots" and/or "repos" list.',
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 2

    if args.report:
        return _run_report(targets, root_errors, args, truncated, discovered_count)

    # Round-2 Minor fix: the sweep path (no --report) previously left
    # truncation invisible too - only `--report --json`'s `"truncated"` key
    # surfaced it. Print the same NOTE style used elsewhere in this file
    # before the per-repo sweep begins, so an operator scrolling sweep
    # output sees it up front rather than having to infer it from a repo
    # count that looks smaller than expected.
    if truncated:
        print(_repo_truncation_note(args.max_repos, discovered_count))

    repos_swept = 0
    repos_errored = 0
    repos_skipped_base = 0

    for target in targets:
        header = str(target.canonical) if target.canonical is not None else target.path
        print(f"== {header} ({target.source}) ==")

        if target.discovery_error is not None:
            print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {target.discovery_error}", file=sys.stderr)
            repos_errored += 1
            continue

        # Round-N Major 1 fix: `base_unresolved_flag` distinguishes "base
        # could not be resolved - reap skipped this run" from a genuinely
        # clean sweep. Before this fix, `_run_repo`'s rc==0 was identical
        # in both cases, so a base-unresolvable repo silently counted as
        # `swept=1`, contradicting both the documented multi-repo exit
        # contract and `--report`'s own identical-condition exit 1 (see
        # `_run_repo`'s own `_base_unresolved_out` docstring for the full
        # rationale). Do NOT count it as `swept` at all - it is reported
        # in its own bucket below, and it still causes exit 1, same as
        # `--report`.
        base_unresolved_flag: List[bool] = []
        rc = _run_repo(str(target.canonical), args, base_unresolved_flag)
        if base_unresolved_flag:
            repos_skipped_base += 1
            continue
        repos_swept += 1
        if rc != 0:
            repos_errored += 1

    print(
        f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: repos={len(targets)} swept={repos_swept} errored={repos_errored} "
        f"root-errors={len(root_errors)} skipped-not-git={skipped_not_git} "
        f"skipped-base-unresolved={repos_skipped_base}"
    )

    if repos_errored or root_errors or repos_skipped_base:
        return 1
    return 0


def main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:
    # Round-N Minor fix: stdout defaults to fully block-buffered whenever
    # it is not a tty (e.g. `> log 2>&1`), while stderr is unbuffered - so
    # the multi-repo sweep's `== repo ==` header (stdout) and that same
    # repo's own errors (stderr, printed deep inside `_run_repo`) can
    # interleave under the WRONG repo when redirected to a single stream.
    # Line-buffering stdout keeps its ordering synced with stderr's own
    # unbuffered writes without having to route every per-repo print
    # through one stream. `reconfigure` is Python 3.7+; guarded for any
    # stdout object that does not support it (e.g. a test harness's
    # captured stream) rather than failing the whole run over a cosmetic
    # ordering guarantee.
    try:
        sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)
    except (AttributeError, ValueError, OSError):
        pass

    args = parse_args(argv)

    if args.init_config:
        # `--init-config` is a standalone scaffolding action, mutually
        # exclusive with every other flag - compare every OTHER field
        # against a fresh no-args default namespace rather than
        # hand-maintaining a second flag list that could drift from
        # `parse_args` above.
        #
        # LIMITATION (round-2 Minor fix, matches the `--init-config` Public
        # API doc above): this value-equality comparison cannot detect an
        # explicitly-passed flag whose value happens to match that flag's
        # own default - e.g. `--init-config --min-age-hours 24` exits 0 and
        # writes, even though `--min-age-hours` was technically combined
        # with `--init-config`. A second hand-maintained "was this field
        # explicitly passed" list would drift from `parse_args` exactly
        # like the flag list this comment already avoids re-introducing,
        # so this is accepted as the documented contract rather than
        # "fixed" with more bookkeeping.
        defaults = parse_args([])
        other_fields_changed = any(
            getattr(args, field) != getattr(defaults, field) for field in vars(args) if field != "init_config"
        )
        if other_fields_changed:
            print("ds-cleanup-worktrees: --init-config cannot be combined with any other flag", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        return _init_config()

    usage_error = _validate_args(args)
    if usage_error is not None:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {usage_error}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    if args.multi_repo:
        return _run_multi_repo(args)

    # Single-repo mode: byte-identical to this tool's behavior before
    # --multi-repo existed. `--repo` is now `action="append"`, but
    # `_validate_args` above already refused more than one entry when
    # `--multi-repo` is absent, so this resolution is exactly the old
    # `args.repo` default-"." behavior for every existing caller
    # (bin/ds-base-sync, hooks/session-start-wrap.sh, this command's own
    # doc), none of which pass --repo more than once.
    resolved_repo_arg = args.repo[0] if args.repo else "."
    try:
        repo = _resolve_repo(resolved_repo_arg)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"ds-cleanup-worktrees: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    return _run_repo(repo, args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
